  {"k":"term","t":"Acceleration structure","s":"A structure that groups a scene's triangles so a ray can skip almost all of them. Ray tracing APIs build it from meshes and instances;…","u":"glossary.html#acceleration-structure","w":"AS ray tracing acceleration structure RayTracingAccelerationStructure"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Acceptance criteria","s":"The conditions, written before work starts, that a result must meet to be accepted. Good ones are measurable, such as a pass under 2 ms on…","u":"glossary.html#acceptance-criteria","w":"definition of done success criteria exit criteria"},
  {"k":"term","t":"ACES","s":"A colour management standard from film production whose output transforms include a tone curve. Games often use fitted approximations of…","u":"glossary.html#aces","w":"Academy Color Encoding System ACES filmic ACES fit RRT ODT ACES 2"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Actor","s":"Unreal Engine's object: anything that can be placed in a level. Its components give it meshes, collision, lights and behaviour, and its…","u":"glossary.html#actor","w":"AActor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Adaptive Probe Volumes","s":"Unity's system in URP and HDRP that places light probes automatically in bricks of 64, closer together where there is more geometry, and…","u":"glossary.html#adaptive-probe-volumes","w":"APV probe volumes Probe Volume Baking Set"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Additive blending","s":"Adding a new colour to the colour already in the image, so it can only brighten. Used for fire, sparks and glows; the result is the same…","u":"glossary.html#additive-blending","w":"additive add blend"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Aerial perspective","s":"Distant objects losing contrast and taking on the sky's colour because of the air between them and the viewer. Renderers apply it from the…","u":"glossary.html#aerial-perspective","w":"atmospheric perspective distance haze Aerial Perspective View Distance Scale"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Agentic workflow","s":"Work in which an AI repeats a loop of planning, acting with tools and checking the result until it reaches a goal or a limit, instead of…","u":"glossary.html#agentic-workflow","w":"agent loop agentic loop autonomous workflow"},
  {"k":"term","t":"AgX","s":"A tone mapping transform by Troy Sobotka that mixes a little of each channel into the others and compresses in log space, so bright…","u":"glossary.html#agx","w":"AgX view transform AgX tonemapper"},
  {"k":"term","t":"AI coding agent","s":"An AI system that works on a codebase in a loop: it reads files, makes changes, runs tools such as the build and the tests, and reports…","u":"glossary.html#ai-coding-agent","w":"coding agent AI agent agent AI assistant"},
  {"k":"term","t":"AI denoiser","s":"A denoiser that is a neural network trained on pairs of noisy and clean images. Offline renderers use one on final frames; real-time…","u":"glossary.html#ai-denoiser","w":"neural denoiser ML denoiser Open Image Denoise OIDN OptiX denoiser FSR Ray Regeneration"},
  {"k":"term","t":"AI-generated asset","s":"A texture, mesh, image, sound or piece of concept art made wholly or partly by a generative AI model. It needs the same technical,…","u":"glossary.html#ai-generated-asset","w":"generated asset AI art generative AI content AI-generated content"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Albedo","s":"The colour a surface reflects under white light, with no lighting or shadow baked in. Light-coloured surfaces bounce more light around a…","u":"glossary.html#albedo","w":"base colour diffuse colour"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Plausible albedo range","s":"The range of base colours real non-metals have, from about 2% (charcoal) to 81% (fresh snow) of light reflected, which validation tools…","u":"glossary.html#albedo-range","w":"PBR validation albedo chart physically plausible values PBR safe range"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Aliasing","s":"False detail that appears when an image is sampled too sparsely for what it shows: stair-stepped edges, broken thin lines, moiré and…","u":"glossary.html#aliasing","w":"sampling artefacts jaggies and shimmer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Alpha","s":"The milestone at which every feature is in the game and playable from start to finish, after which no new features are started. Studios…","u":"glossary.html#alpha","w":"feature complete"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Alpha blending","s":"Mixing a new colour over the existing one in proportion to its alpha: alpha × new + (1 − alpha) × old. The result depends on draw order,…","u":"glossary.html#alpha-blending","w":"alpha blend translucent transparent over"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Alpha test","s":"Discarding pixels whose alpha falls below a threshold, typically 0.5, and drawing the rest fully opaque. Cut-outs such as leaves and…","u":"glossary.html#alpha-test","w":"alpha cutout alpha clip alpha clipping alpha scissor masked"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Alpha-tested foliage","s":"Leaves and grass drawn as flat cards whose texture alpha cuts out the shape. The pixel shader must run across the whole card to decide…","u":"glossary.html#alpha-tested-foliage","w":"cut-out foliage masked foliage alpha cards foliage cards"},
  {"k":"term","t":"ALU-bound","s":"A GPU pass limited by the arithmetic in its shaders, such as long material graphs or many lights per pixel. Only less maths per pixel or…","u":"glossary.html#alu-bound","w":"compute-bound math-bound shader-bound arithmetic-bound"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ambient occlusion","s":"How much of the surrounding sky or environment a point can see. Creases, corners and contact points see less of it, so they receive less…","u":"glossary.html#ambient-occlusion","w":"AO"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ambient term","s":"A constant amount of light added to every point to stand in for light bouncing around a scene. It has no direction, so it flattens shapes;…","u":"glossary.html#ambient-term","w":"ambient flat ambient ambient colour"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Animation compression","s":"Shrinking stored animation by dropping keys that interpolation can rebuild and storing the rest with fewer bits, trading memory for small…","u":"glossary.html#animation-compression","w":"keyframe reduction bone compression curve compression"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Animation system","s":"The engine system that plays, blends and adjusts animations, such as a character's walk and run cycles, and poses the skeleton every frame.","u":"glossary.html#animation-system","w":"animation Animator Animation Blueprint AnimationTree"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Anisotropic filtering","s":"Taking several samples along the long axis of a pixel's stretched footprint, so surfaces seen at a grazing angle, such as floors and…","u":"glossary.html#anisotropic-filtering","w":"AF aniso 16× AF Aniso Level"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Anti-aliasing","s":"Any technique that brings each pixel closer to the true share of what it covers, by taking more samples, smoothing the finished image, or…","u":"glossary.html#anti-aliasing","w":"AA antialiasing Anti-Aliasing Method"},
  {"k":"term","t":"AMD Anti-Lag 2","s":"AMD's in-game latency reducer for Radeon GPUs: it aligns CPU and GPU work so frames do not queue, cutting the time from input to screen.…","u":"glossary.html#anti-lag","w":"Anti-Lag Radeon Anti-Lag 2 Anti-Lag 2"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Archetype","s":"In an entity component system, the group of entities that have exactly the same component types. They are stored together in fixed-size…","u":"glossary.html#archetype","w":"chunk"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Area light","s":"A light that shines from a shape with real size, such as a rectangle or a disc. It gives soft shadows and broad highlights, and costs more…","u":"glossary.html#area-light","w":"rect light Rect Light AreaLight3D disc light tube light"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Art director","s":"The person who sets a game's visual direction and approves whether art, lighting and effects match it.","u":"glossary.html#art-director","w":"AD"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Asset","s":"Any piece of content a game is built from, such as a model, texture, material, sound, animation or level, once the engine has imported it…","u":"glossary.html#asset","w":"game asset content"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cooking","s":"Converting imported assets into the exact form one platform loads: textures compressed into that GPU's formats, shaders compiled, and…","u":"glossary.html#asset-cooking","w":"cook asset cooking content build"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Asset database","s":"The engine's record of every asset under a stable ID, with its type, location and dependencies, so references survive renames and moves.","u":"glossary.html#asset-database","w":"asset registry AssetDatabase Asset Registry"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Import","s":"The engine step that reads an exported file and converts it into the engine's own representation, using import settings stored alongside it.","u":"glossary.html#asset-import","w":"asset import importer reimport"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Asset system","s":"The engine system that finds, loads, tracks and unloads a game's content files, such as meshes, textures and sounds, so other systems can…","u":"glossary.html#asset-system","w":"asset manager resource manager asset database"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Asset validation","s":"Automatic checks that every asset follows the project's rules, such as naming, triangle and texture-size budgets, and references only to…","u":"glossary.html#asset-validation","w":"data validation asset checks content validation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"ASTC","s":"A texture format designed by Arm and AMD, used mainly on phones and Apple devices. Every block is 128 bits, but each texture picks a block…","u":"glossary.html#astc","w":"Adaptive Scalable Texture Compression ASTC 4x4 ASTC 6x6 ASTC HDR"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Async compute","s":"Running compute shader passes on a second GPU queue at the same time as drawing, so work that uses different parts of the GPU, such as AO…","u":"glossary.html#async-compute","w":"asynchronous compute AsyncCompute compute queue Asynchronous Execution"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Asynchronous loading","s":"Reading and preparing assets on other threads while the game keeps running, leaving only small final steps for the game thread, so loading…","u":"glossary.html#async-loading","w":"async loading background loading RequestAsyncLoad LoadAssetAsync load_threaded_request"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Attenuation radius","s":"The distance beyond which the renderer ignores a light, so it does not cost shading work across the whole world. Engines fade the light…","u":"glossary.html#attenuation-radius","w":"range Range light radius Attenuation Radius"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Attribute interpolation","s":"Blending the values a vertex shader writes for a triangle's three corners, such as colour, UVs and normals, to get a value at each covered…","u":"glossary.html#attribute-interpolation","w":"interpolation varying interpolation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Audio engine","s":"The engine system that plays and mixes sounds, places them in 3D space relative to the listener, and streams music and dialogue.","u":"glossary.html#audio-engine","w":"audio sound system audio middleware"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Auto-exposure","s":"Exposure set from the frame itself: the renderer measures the scene's brightness and moves exposure towards a target over time, like eyes…","u":"glossary.html#auto-exposure","w":"eye adaptation automatic exposure adaptive exposure Auto Exposure Histogram"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Backface culling","s":"Skipping triangles that face away from the camera. The GPU decides which way a triangle faces from the order of its corners on screen.","u":"glossary.html#backface-culling","w":"face culling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cage","s":"An inflated copy of the low-poly mesh that baking rays start from, travelling inwards to find the high-poly surface. Its distance decides…","u":"glossary.html#bake-cage","w":"bake cage projection cage envelope max ray distance"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Baked lighting","s":"Lighting calculated ahead of time, usually in the editor, and stored in textures or probes. Cheap at runtime and high quality, but it…","u":"glossary.html#baked-lighting","w":"precomputed lighting light baking"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Baking","s":"Transferring detail into a texture ahead of time, most often from a high-poly sculpt onto a low-poly mesh as normal and ambient occlusion…","u":"glossary.html#baking","w":"texture baking mesh map baking normal map baking"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bandwidth-bound","s":"A GPU pass limited by how many bytes it moves to and from memory, common for full-screen passes over large images. Smaller formats, fewer…","u":"glossary.html#bandwidth-bound","w":"memory-bound memory bandwidth bound VRAM-bound"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Barycentric coordinates","s":"Three weights, one per corner of a triangle, that say how close a point is to each corner. They add up to 1, and a weight below 0 means…","u":"glossary.html#barycentric-coordinates","w":"barycentrics barycentric weights"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Batching","s":"Any technique that turns many draw requests into fewer or cheaper ones, such as merging meshes, sorting draws by material or instancing.","u":"glossary.html#batching","w":"draw call batching batch"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BC1","s":"A block-compressed format storing RGB, with optional on-or-off alpha, at 4 bits per pixel: two endpoint colours and a 2-bit index per…","u":"glossary.html#bc1","w":"DXT1"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BC3","s":"A block-compressed format at 8 bits per pixel: RGB stored as in BC1, plus a separately compressed alpha channel. Used for colour textures…","u":"glossary.html#bc3","w":"DXT5"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BC4","s":"A single-channel block-compressed format at 4 bits per pixel, with eight levels between two endpoints in each block. Used for roughness,…","u":"glossary.html#bc4","w":"ATI1 RGTC1 R Compressed BC4"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BC5","s":"A two-channel block-compressed format at 8 bits per pixel, each channel stored like BC4. The standard format for normal maps, which keep X…","u":"glossary.html#bc5","w":"ATI2 3Dc RGTC2 RG Compressed BC5"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BC6H","s":"A block-compressed format for high dynamic range RGB at 8 bits per pixel, storing 16-bit floating-point colour. Used for skies, light…","u":"glossary.html#bc6h","w":"BC6 HDR Compressed"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BC7","s":"A high-quality block-compressed format for RGB or RGBA at 8 bits per pixel. Each 4 × 4 block picks one of eight modes, some with up to…","u":"glossary.html#bc7","w":"BPTC"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BCn","s":"The family of block-compressed texture formats numbered BC1 to BC7, used on PCs and consoles. Each stores 4 × 4 texel blocks in 8 or 16…","u":"glossary.html#bcn","w":"BC formats block compression formats DXTn BPTC RGTC"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bent normal","s":"The average direction of open sky seen from a point. Where a surface is partly enclosed it leans away from the occluders, and engines use…","u":"glossary.html#bent-normal","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Beta","s":"The milestone at which a game is feature and content complete, and the remaining work is fixing bugs, optimising and polishing before…","u":"glossary.html#beta","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bilateral filter","s":"A blur that only mixes neighbouring pixels at a similar depth and facing. It smooths noise without smearing one object's values onto…","u":"glossary.html#bilateral-filter","w":"depth-aware blur edge-aware blur"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bilinear filtering","s":"Blending the four texels around the sample point, each weighted by how close it is, so magnified textures look smooth instead of blocky.","u":"glossary.html#bilinear-filtering","w":"bilinear linear filtering Bi-linear"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Billboard","s":"A flat card that turns to face the camera every frame. Used for impostors, distant trees and many particles.","u":"glossary.html#billboard","w":"camera-facing card Billboard Renderer Billboard Mode"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bind pose","s":"The pose a skinned mesh was modelled and weighted in. Skinning measures each vertex relative to its bones in this pose, then applies how…","u":"glossary.html#bind-pose","w":"rest pose reference pose T-pose A-pose inverse bind matrix"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Binning","s":"The first phase of a tile-based GPU's render pass: every triangle is positioned and added to a list for each screen tile it touches, and…","u":"glossary.html#binning","w":"binning pass tiling pass geometry phase bins tile lists"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bit depth","s":"How many bits store each colour channel. 8 bits give 256 levels per channel; 10 bits give 1,024, which HDR signals need to avoid visible…","u":"glossary.html#bit-depth","w":"8-bit 10-bit bits per channel bpc"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bitangent","s":"The third direction of the tangent frame, along the texture's vertical axis and at right angles to the normal and tangent. It is usually…","u":"glossary.html#bitangent","w":"binormal"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bits per pixel","s":"How much memory a texture format uses per texel, on average: 32 for RGBA8, 8 for BC7, 4 for BC1. Multiply by width × height and divide by…","u":"glossary.html#bits-per-pixel","w":"bpp bits per texel bpt bit rate"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BLAS","s":"The acceleration structure for one mesh's triangles, built in the mesh's own coordinate space. Many instances of the mesh share one BLAS.","u":"glossary.html#blas","w":"bottom-level acceleration structure bottom-level AS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Blend mode","s":"The rule the GPU uses to combine a new colour with the colour already in the image: replace it, mix by alpha, add, multiply and so on.…","u":"glossary.html#blend-mode","w":"blend state blending mode blend equation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Blend shape","s":"A stored version of a mesh in another shape, such as a smile, kept as offsets from the original. Weights from 0 to 1 mix any number of…","u":"glossary.html#blend-shape","w":"morph target shape key blendshape"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Blinn-Phong","s":"Jim Blinn's 1977 version of Phong's model, which measures the half vector against the normal instead of the reflection against the view.…","u":"glossary.html#blinn-phong","w":"Blinn-Phong specular Blinn"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Block compression","s":"Storing a texture as small blocks of texels, usually 4 × 4, each in the same small number of bytes, so the GPU can find and decode any…","u":"glossary.html#block-compression","w":"texture compression GPU texture compression DXT S3TC"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bloom","s":"A glow around very bright light, made by blurring the brightest parts of the HDR image at several sizes and adding a little of the result…","u":"glossary.html#bloom","w":"glow Glow veil glare"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Blueprint","s":"Unreal Engine's visual scripting system. A Blueprint class bundles an Actor's components, settings and node-based logic into an asset that…","u":"glossary.html#blueprint","w":"Blueprint class Blueprint Visual Scripting BP"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bokeh","s":"The look of out-of-focus areas, especially small bright lights, which take the shape of the lens aperture: hexagons for six straight…","u":"glossary.html#bokeh","w":"bokeh shape aperture shape Blade Count"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bone","s":"One node of a skeleton: a transform with a parent. Animation sets its rotation, and sometimes its position and scale, and the vertices…","u":"glossary.html#bone","w":"joint"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bottleneck","s":"The one part of the work that sets how long a frame takes, such as the GPU or the game thread. Speeding up anything else leaves the frame…","u":"glossary.html#bottleneck","w":"limiter limiting factor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bounce","s":"One reflection of light off a surface on its way from a light to the eye. Each bounce carries only part of the light before it, because…","u":"glossary.html#bounce","w":"light bounce indirect bounce bounces"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bounding volume","s":"A simple shape, usually a box aligned with the world axes (AABB) or a sphere, that fully encloses an object. Culling tests these shapes…","u":"glossary.html#bounding-volume","w":"bounds bounding box AABB axis-aligned bounding box bounding sphere OBB"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Box projection","s":"Correcting a reflection probe by first intersecting each reflected ray with a box matching the room, then reading the cubemap towards that…","u":"glossary.html#box-projection","w":"parallax-corrected cubemap parallax correction proxy volume Box Projection"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BRDF","s":"The function that says how much of the light arriving from one direction a surface sends towards another. Most engines use Lambert diffuse…","u":"glossary.html#brdf","w":"bidirectional reflectance distribution function reflectance model"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Brief","s":"The written request that starts a piece of AI work: the context, the problem or goal, the limits, what to hand back and how the result…","u":"glossary.html#brief","w":"prompt task description ticket work request"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Constraints","s":"The limits a solution must respect: frame and memory budgets, platforms, what must not change in the look or the code, files out of scope…","u":"glossary.html#brief-constraints","w":"requirements limits guardrails must not change"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Context (in a brief)","s":"The facts in a brief that an AI cannot guess: the engine and its exact version, the render pipeline or rendering path, the target…","u":"glossary.html#brief-context","w":"project context environment setup"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Budget allocation","s":"Dividing a frame budget into milliseconds for each system and pass, such as 2 ms for shadows or 3 ms for animation, so each team owns a…","u":"glossary.html#budget-allocation","w":"per-system budget budget split millisecond budget performance budget"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Build","s":"A runnable copy of the game for one platform: compiled code plus cooked assets, packed into a few large files.","u":"glossary.html#build","w":"game build packaged build package"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bump mapping","s":"Tilting the shading normal per pixel using the slopes of a height map, so a surface looks bumpy without changing shape. Introduced by…","u":"glossary.html#bump-mapping","w":"bump map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"BVH","s":"A tree of boxes: each box encloses the boxes below it, and the smallest boxes hold a few triangles. A ray that misses a box skips…","u":"glossary.html#bvh","w":"bounding volume hierarchy BVH tree"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Refit","s":"Updating a BVH after vertices move by regrowing its boxes to fit, keeping the same tree. It is much faster than a rebuild, but the boxes…","u":"glossary.html#bvh-refit","w":"BVH refit BVH update acceleration structure update PERFORM_UPDATE"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Candela","s":"The unit of how strong a light source is in one direction. A candle is about 1 candela, and a point light of 1 candela gives about 12.6…","u":"glossary.html#candela","w":"cd candelas luminous intensity"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Candy-wrapper artefact","s":"The collapse of a twisting joint under linear blend skinning, which pinches the mesh to a thin waist like the twisted ends of a sweet…","u":"glossary.html#candy-wrapper","w":"candy wrapper joint collapse volume loss"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Capsule shadows","s":"Soft character shadows in Unreal Engine cast from a set of capsules attached to the character's bones, mainly to ground characters in…","u":"glossary.html#capsule-shadows","w":"Capsule Direct Shadow Capsule Indirect Shadow Shadow Physics Asset"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cascade split","s":"The distance from the camera where one shadow cascade ends and the next begins. Splits are placed evenly, logarithmically or by a blend of…","u":"glossary.html#cascade-split","w":"split distance Split 1 Distribution Exponent split scheme"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cascaded shadow maps","s":"Splitting the camera's view into a few distance ranges and giving each its own shadow map, so nearby ground gets small texels and distant…","u":"glossary.html#cascaded-shadow-maps","w":"CSM shadow cascades cascades PSSM parallel-split shadow maps"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Caustics","s":"The bright, moving network of light that a wavy water surface focuses onto the floor below it. Games usually project an animated or…","u":"glossary.html#caustics","w":"water caustics caustic pattern"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Certification","s":"The platform holder's testing of a submitted build against its published requirements before a game or update may be released. A failed…","u":"glossary.html#certification","w":"cert submission TRC TCR Xbox Requirements XR Lotcheck VRC"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Channel packing","s":"Storing up to four single-value maps, such as occlusion, roughness and metallic, in the red, green, blue and alpha channels of one…","u":"glossary.html#channel-packing","w":"packed texture ORM map mask map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Chromatic aberration","s":"Red and blue fringes along high-contrast edges, growing towards the edges of the frame, imitating a lens that does not focus all colours…","u":"glossary.html#chromatic-aberration","w":"CA colour fringing color fringing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Circle of confusion","s":"The disc an out-of-focus point spreads into on the sensor. Its size follows from the lens's focal length, aperture and focus distance and…","u":"glossary.html#circle-of-confusion","w":"CoC blur circle blur disc"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Clip space","s":"The four numbers (x, y, z, w) a vertex shader outputs for each vertex after the projection matrix. The GPU uses them to cut away anything…","u":"glossary.html#clip-space","w":"homogeneous clip space clip coordinates HClip"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Clipmap","s":"A stack of textures centred on the camera, each covering twice the area of the one inside it at the same resolution, so detail falls off…","u":"glossary.html#clipmap","w":"clipmap level clipmap levels"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cloth shading","s":"A shading model for fabrics such as velvet and wool that adds a soft sheen where loose fibres catch light at grazing angles, which the…","u":"glossary.html#cloth-shading","w":"sheen fabric shading Cloth shading model"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cluster LOD","s":"Choosing level of detail per small cluster of triangles instead of per object, so near and far parts of one mesh get different detail.…","u":"glossary.html#cluster-lod","w":"cluster-based LOD cluster hierarchy"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Clustered shading","s":"Light culling that cuts the view into a 3D grid of clusters, screen tiles sliced in depth, and lists the lights reaching each cluster.…","u":"glossary.html#clustered-shading","w":"clustered lighting clustered forward clustered deferred"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Colour banding","s":"Visible stripes or steps in what should be a smooth gradient, such as a sky or fog, because too few levels are available to store it.","u":"glossary.html#colour-banding","w":"banding contouring posterisation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Colour bleeding","s":"One surface's colour tinting its neighbours through bounced light, such as a red wall turning the floor beside it pink. Too strong usually…","u":"glossary.html#colour-bleeding","w":"color bleeding colour bleed"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Colour channel","s":"One of the separate numbers stored for each pixel, such as red, green, blue or alpha (opacity). Each channel is stored with a fixed number…","u":"glossary.html#colour-channel","w":"channel R, G, B, A alpha channel"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Colour gamut","s":"The range of colours a colour space or display can produce, set by its red, green and blue primaries. Rec. 2020, used by HDR10, covers far…","u":"glossary.html#colour-gamut","w":"gamut primaries Rec. 2020 DCI-P3"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Colour grading","s":"Adjusting the frame's colours for mood and consistency: contrast, saturation, tints for shadows and highlights, curves. Done on…","u":"glossary.html#colour-grading","w":"color grading grading colour correction look"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Colour temperature","s":"A light's tint described as the temperature, in kelvin, of a glowing object of that colour. A candle flame is about 1,850 K and orange;…","u":"glossary.html#colour-temperature","w":"color temperature Kelvin Temperature CCT"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Command buffer","s":"A recorded list of GPU instructions, such as which settings to use, which buffers to read and what to draw. The CPU builds it, then hands…","u":"glossary.html#command-buffer","w":"command list"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Component","s":"A piece of data or behaviour attached to an object, such as a mesh, a collider, a light or a script. Objects are built by combining…","u":"glossary.html#component","w":"Actor Component MonoBehaviour fragment"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Compute shader","s":"A GPU program that is not tied to drawing triangles. Renderers use compute shaders for post effects, AO, culling and simulation.","u":"glossary.html#compute-shader","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Compute unit","s":"The repeated building block of a GPU: a set of lanes with its own scheduler, registers, small cache and texture units. A GPU has anywhere…","u":"glossary.html#compute-unit","w":"CU SM streaming multiprocessor GPU core shader core"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Concept phase","s":"The first phase of a game project, where a few people define the idea, audience, platforms, scope and rough cost. It ends with a pitch and…","u":"glossary.html#concept-phase","w":"concept ideation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Constant buffer","s":"A small block of values the CPU sets for a draw, such as a material's tint or the camera's position, that stays the same for every vertex…","u":"glossary.html#constant-buffer","w":"uniform buffer cbuffer UBO uniforms"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Contact shadows","s":"Small shadows found by stepping a short way from each pixel towards the light through the depth buffer. They restore shadows where objects…","u":"glossary.html#contact-shadows","w":"screen-space shadows Contact Shadow Length screen space contact shadows"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Content complete","s":"The point at which every level, asset, line of dialogue and piece of interface is in the game in final or near-final form, so only fixes…","u":"glossary.html#content-complete","w":"asset complete"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Content pipeline","s":"The chain of tools, rules and people that carries art from the programs artists make it in, through the engine, into the files that ship…","u":"glossary.html#content-pipeline","w":"asset pipeline art pipeline"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Continuous integration","s":"Build machines that pick up every submitted change, build and test it automatically, and report failures to the team.","u":"glossary.html#continuous-integration","w":"CI build server build farm"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cook-Torrance","s":"The microfacet specular model physically based renderers use: a facet distribution D (usually GGX), a Fresnel term F and a shadowing term…","u":"glossary.html#cook-torrance","w":"Cook-Torrance microfacet model microfacet specular"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cooked asset","s":"An asset converted for one platform: compressed, resized and stripped of editor-only data. It loads fast and is not meant to be edited.","u":"glossary.html#cooked-asset","w":"cooked content runtime asset"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Coordinate space","s":"An origin plus a set of axes that positions are measured from. The same corner of a model has different numbers in each space.","u":"glossary.html#coordinate-space","w":"space coordinate system coordinate frame frame of reference"},
  {"k":"term","t":"CPU","s":"The general-purpose processor that runs game logic and the engine, and prepares the work the GPU will do each frame.","u":"glossary.html#cpu","w":"central processing unit processor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"CPU-bound","s":"A frame whose time is set by a CPU thread, such as the game thread or the render thread, while the GPU waits. Lowering the resolution does…","u":"glossary.html#cpu-bound","w":"CPU bound CPU-limited game-thread bound render-thread bound"},
  {"k":"term","t":"CPU cache","s":"Small, fast memory beside each CPU core. Data arrives in blocks called cache lines, usually 64 bytes; needing data that is not there is a…","u":"glossary.html#cpu-cache","w":"cache line cache miss"},
  {"k":"term","t":"CPU profiler","s":"A tool that records what each CPU thread did over time, function by function and frame by frame, so you can see which thread and which…","u":"glossary.html#cpu-profiler","w":"timing capture sampling profiler instrumenting profiler CPU Usage module"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Critical path","s":"The longest chain of work in a frame that has to happen in order because each step needs the one before. However many cores a job system…","u":"glossary.html#critical-path","w":"longest dependency chain serial path"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shader cross-compilation","s":"Compiling one shader source into the different formats each graphics API needs, such as DXIL for Direct3D 12, SPIR-V for Vulkan and a…","u":"glossary.html#cross-compilation","w":"cross-compiling DXC SPIRV-Cross shader translation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cross product","s":"An operation on two 3D vectors that gives a third vector at right angles to both, as long as the area of the parallelogram they span.…","u":"glossary.html#cross-product","w":"vector product cross"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Crowd rendering","s":"Drawing hundreds or thousands of animated characters at once, usually with instancing, baked vertex animation, simple skeletons and…","u":"glossary.html#crowd-rendering","w":"crowds crowd system animation instancing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Crunch","s":"Sustained long working hours used to hit a deadline. It raises output briefly, then costs mistakes, health and experienced people leaving.","u":"glossary.html#crunch","w":"crunch time crunch mode"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Cubemap","s":"Six square images forming the faces of a cube, read with a direction instead of UV coordinates. Skies and reflections use them.","u":"glossary.html#cubemap","w":"cube map cube texture TextureCube"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Culling","s":"Finding objects, or parts of them, that cannot affect the image and skipping them before they cost any drawing work. Frustum, occlusion,…","u":"glossary.html#culling","w":"visibility culling visibility determination"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Custom engine","s":"An engine a studio builds and maintains for its own games, such as Guerrilla's Decima. It can fit the studio's needs exactly, but it needs…","u":"glossary.html#custom-engine","w":"in-house engine proprietary engine"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Day-one patch","s":"An update released on or before launch day that players download over the gold master. It carries fixes made after the release build was…","u":"glossary.html#day-one-patch","w":"launch patch day-zero patch"},
  {"k":"term","t":"DCC tool","s":"A program artists make game content in, such as Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Houdini, ZBrush or Substance 3D Painter. Each saves its own…","u":"glossary.html#dcc-tool","w":"digital content creation tool content creation software"},
  {"k":"term","t":"DDGI","s":"A grid of probes that trace rays every frame and store both the light they see and the distance to the nearest surface in each direction.…","u":"glossary.html#ddgi","w":"dynamic diffuse global illumination RTXGI irradiance probes with visibility"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Debug marker","s":"A name the game wraps around a group of GPU commands, such as Shadow depths or Bloom, so capture tools can show a frame as named passes…","u":"glossary.html#debug-marker","w":"debug region event marker PIX event draw event user marker named event"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Deferred decal","s":"A decal drawn as a box that projects onto the surfaces already stored inside it, changing their base colour, normals or roughness before…","u":"glossary.html#deferred-decal","w":"DBuffer decal D-buffer decal screen-space decal decal projector"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Deferred rendering","s":"A way of organising a frame that first writes each pixel's surface properties to a G-buffer and lights them in a later pass. It handles…","u":"glossary.html#deferred-rendering","w":"deferred shading"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Delta time","s":"The time that passed since the previous frame. Update code multiplies speeds by it so that objects move the same distance per second at…","u":"glossary.html#delta-time","w":"dt variable timestep DeltaSeconds Time.deltaTime"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Denoising","s":"Turning a noisy, under-sampled image into a clean one by blending neighbouring pixels and previous frames. Most real-time AO, GI and ray…","u":"glossary.html#denoising","w":"denoiser"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Depth buffer","s":"A screen-sized image storing, for every pixel, how far the nearest visible surface is from the camera. It is the renderer's only record of…","u":"glossary.html#depth-buffer","w":"z-buffer depth texture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Depth of field","s":"The range of distances that look sharp through a lens, and the effect that blurs everything nearer or farther, by an amount computed from…","u":"glossary.html#depth-of-field","w":"DOF DoF focus blur Cinematic DOF"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Depth pre-pass","s":"Drawing the opaque scene once with depth only, then again with full shading where each fragment must match the stored depth, so every…","u":"glossary.html#depth-prepass","w":"depth prepass Z-prepass early Z pass depth priming"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Depth test","s":"The GPU's per-pixel check that keeps a new fragment only if it is nearer than the distance already stored in the depth buffer. It is what…","u":"glossary.html#depth-test","w":"z-test ZTest depth comparison"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Depth write","s":"Storing a fragment's depth in the depth buffer after it passes the depth test. Opaque surfaces write depth; blended transparent surfaces…","u":"glossary.html#depth-write","w":"z-write ZWrite depth draw mode"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Derived data cache","s":"A cache of processed versions of assets, such as compressed textures and compiled shaders, so work done once on one machine can be reused…","u":"glossary.html#derived-data-cache","w":"DDC shared DDC cache server Unity Accelerator"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Detail normal map","s":"A small, tiling normal map of fine grain blended over a material's main normal map, adding close-up detail for almost no memory.","u":"glossary.html#detail-normal-map","w":"detail map detail normal micro normal map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Devkit","s":"A development version of a console or device, supplied by the platform holder under contract, that studios use to build, run and debug…","u":"glossary.html#devkit","w":"development kit dev kit"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Diffuse reflection","s":"Light that a surface scatters evenly in all directions, so it looks the same from any viewing angle. Matte paint is mostly diffuse.","u":"glossary.html#diffuse","w":"diffuse"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Diffuse GI","s":"Bounced light scattered evenly by matte surfaces. It does not change as the camera moves and varies smoothly, so it can be baked into…","u":"glossary.html#diffuse-gi","w":"diffuse indirect lighting indirect diffuse diffuse global illumination"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Direct light","s":"Light that travels straight from a light source to a surface without bouncing off anything else first.","u":"glossary.html#direct-light","w":"direct lighting"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Directional light","s":"A light so far away that its rays arrive parallel and equally strong everywhere, used for the sun and the moon. It has a direction and a…","u":"glossary.html#directional-light","w":"sun light Directional Light DirectionalLight3D"},
  {"k":"term","t":"DirectStorage","s":"Microsoft's Windows API for fast loading from NVMe drives. Games submit many reads in batches instead of one at a time, and can have the…","u":"glossary.html#directstorage","w":"Microsoft DirectStorage DStorage"},
  {"k":"term","t":"DirectX 12","s":"Microsoft's explicit graphics API for Windows and Xbox. The engine manages GPU memory and synchronisation itself, in exchange for much…","u":"glossary.html#directx-12","w":"Direct3D 12 D3D12 DX12"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Discrete GPU","s":"A GPU on its own card with its own VRAM, connected to the CPU through a PCI Express slot. Its VRAM is very fast, but data has to cross a…","u":"glossary.html#discrete-gpu","w":"dedicated GPU graphics card dGPU"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Disocclusion","s":"Pixels that become visible this frame after being hidden last frame. Temporal techniques have no history for them, so they often show…","u":"glossary.html#disocclusion","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Displacement","s":"Moving each vertex along its normal by an amount read from a height texture, so the surface's real shape and silhouette change. It needs…","u":"glossary.html#displacement","w":"displacement mapping vertex displacement height displacement"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Displacement mapping","s":"Moving a mesh's real vertices along their normals by a height map, so the shape and its outline change. It needs enough vertices, often…","u":"glossary.html#displacement-mapping","w":"displacement vertex displacement true displacement"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Display-referred","s":"Describing values as what the screen should show, from black up to its white or peak brightness. Values after tone mapping are…","u":"glossary.html#display-referred","w":"display referred output-referred"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Distance culling","s":"Skipping objects beyond a set distance from the camera, usually shorter for smaller objects, because they would cover only a few pixels.","u":"glossary.html#distance-culling","w":"cull distance draw distance max draw distance visibility range Cull Distance Volume"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Distance-field shadows","s":"Shadows found by tracing a ray towards the light through the scene's signed distance fields. The closest the ray passes to anything gives…","u":"glossary.html#distance-field-shadows","w":"distance field soft shadows DF shadows ray traced distance field shadows"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Distance fog","s":"Fog that hides the scene by distance from the camera alone, the same in every direction and at every height. One formula per pixel; the…","u":"glossary.html#distance-fog","w":"depth fog linear fog exponential fog Exponential Squared Fog (Lighting window)"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Dithering","s":"Adding a tiny amount of noise before reducing an image to fewer levels, so hard steps become fine grain that the eye averages into a…","u":"glossary.html#dithering","w":"dither debanding"},
  {"k":"term","t":"DLSS","s":"NVIDIA's family of machine-learned rendering features for GeForce RTX GPUs: a temporal upscaler (Super Resolution, or DLAA at native…","u":"glossary.html#dlss","w":"Deep Learning Super Sampling DLSS Super Resolution DLAA DLSS 4 DLSS 4.5"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Dot product","s":"Multiply two vectors' matching components and add the results. For unit vectors the answer is the cosine of the angle between them: 1 when…","u":"glossary.html#dot-product","w":"scalar product dot"},
  {"k":"term","t":"DOTS","s":"Unity's data-oriented toolset, built around the Entities package, its entity component system. GameObjects are converted into entities in…","u":"glossary.html#dots","w":"Unity Entities Data-Oriented Technology Stack"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Double buffering","s":"Using two framebuffers: the display reads the front one while the GPU draws into the back one, then the two swap roles.","u":"glossary.html#double-buffering","w":"front buffer back buffer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Draw call","s":"One instruction from the CPU telling the GPU to draw a set of triangles with a given mesh, material and settings. Each one has a CPU cost,…","u":"glossary.html#draw-call","w":"draw DrawIndexed"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Draw call budget","s":"The number of draw calls per frame a project allows on each platform, set from how many the platform's CPU can record within the frame…","u":"glossary.html#draw-call-budget","w":"draw call limit draw call target"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Graphics driver","s":"Software from the GPU maker that turns graphics API commands into instructions for one particular GPU and compiles shaders into its…","u":"glossary.html#driver","w":"driver GPU driver display driver"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Dual-quaternion skinning","s":"A skinning method that blends the bones' rotations and then rotates the vertex, so twisted and bent joints keep their thickness. It…","u":"glossary.html#dual-quaternion-skinning","w":"DQS dual quaternion blending Preserve Volume"},
  {"k":"term","t":"DXR","s":"Microsoft's ray tracing API for DirectX 12, announced in 2018: two-level acceleration structures, ray tracing shaders and, from tier 1.1,…","u":"glossary.html#dxr","w":"DirectX Raytracing DXR 1.1 DXR 1.2"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Dynamic batching","s":"Combining small moving meshes on the CPU every frame by transforming their vertices into one shared buffer and drawing it once. The…","u":"glossary.html#dynamic-batching","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Dynamic buffer","s":"A GPU buffer the CPU rewrites often, sometimes every frame. Used for particles, UI and procedurally generated geometry.","u":"glossary.html#dynamic-buffer","w":"dynamic vertex buffer streaming buffer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Dynamic light","s":"A light computed every frame, so it can move, change and cast shadows from moving objects. Each one adds GPU time to every frame it is in…","u":"glossary.html#dynamic-light","w":"movable light real-time light Movable Realtime"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Dynamic resolution","s":"Lowering and raising the internal resolution every few frames, from measured GPU time, to stay within the frame budget. An upscaler then…","u":"glossary.html#dynamic-resolution","w":"dynamic resolution scaling DRS dynamic res Allow Dynamic Resolution r.DynamicRes"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Early-Z","s":"A depth test the GPU runs before the pixel shader, so fragments already hidden behind something drawn earlier are thrown away without…","u":"glossary.html#early-z","w":"early depth test early depth testing early-Z rejection Hi-Z"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Entity component system","s":"A way to organise a game world as plain data: entities are ID numbers, components are data in tightly packed arrays, and systems are code…","u":"glossary.html#ecs","w":"ECS data-oriented design"},
  {"k":"term","t":"System","s":"In an entity component system, code that runs over every entity with a particular set of components, for example moving everything that…","u":"glossary.html#ecs-system","w":"ECS system processor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Edge aliasing","s":"Stair-step edges, broken-up thin objects and crawling pixels caused by testing coverage at only one point per pixel. Anti-aliasing…","u":"glossary.html#edge-aliasing","w":"jaggies stair-stepping geometric aliasing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Editor","s":"The engine's application for making a game: placing objects, importing content, tuning materials and testing. It stays on the team's…","u":"glossary.html#editor","w":"level editor authoring tools Unreal Editor Unity Editor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Emissive surface","s":"A surface whose material gives off its own light, such as a screen or a neon sign. It always glows, but lights other surfaces only through…","u":"glossary.html#emissive","w":"emission emissive material Emissive Color self-illumination"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Energy conservation","s":"The rule that a surface cannot send out more light than reaches it. PBR keeps it by dimming highlights as they spread and by not counting…","u":"glossary.html#energy-conservation","w":"energy conserving"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Engine programmer","s":"A programmer who works on the engine's foundations, such as memory, threads, file loading, streaming and platform support, which every…","u":"glossary.html#engine-programmer","w":"engine engineer core programmer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Entity","s":"In an entity component system, an ID number that ties a set of components together. It has no data or behaviour of its own.","u":"glossary.html#entity","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Environment map","s":"A picture of the light arriving at one point from every direction, usually stored as a cubemap and read by direction. Exact for distant…","u":"glossary.html#environment-map","w":"env map reflection map environment cubemap HDRI"},
  {"k":"term","t":"ETC2","s":"A block-compressed texture format supported by every OpenGL ES 3.0 and Vulkan phone: 4 bits per pixel for RGB and 8 for RGBA. Its EAC…","u":"glossary.html#etc2","w":"EAC ETC2 RGB ETC2 RGBA ETC"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Evidence","s":"The material that shows what happened: frame captures, screenshots from fixed cameras, timings, logs and test results, attached to a brief…","u":"glossary.html#evidence","w":"attachments artefacts proof before and after"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Exposure","s":"The multiplier applied to scene light before the tone curve. It decides which brightness lands in the middle of the picture, as a camera's…","u":"glossary.html#exposure","w":"Exposure Compensation camera exposure Post Exposure"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Exposure value","s":"A log scale for exposure on which each step doubles or halves the light. EV100 is measured as for ISO 100 film; Unity's guide puts sunlit…","u":"glossary.html#exposure-value","w":"EV EV100 stop stops"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Extinction","s":"The light lost along a path through participating media, absorbed or scattered out of the path. The share that survives falls…","u":"glossary.html#extinction","w":"out-scattering absorption Beer-Lambert law Beer's law transmittance"},
  {"k":"term","t":"F0","s":"How much light a surface reflects when seen straight on. About 4% for most non-metals; for metals it is the base colour, from about 56%…","u":"glossary.html#f0","w":"reflectance at normal incidence specular colour base reflectivity"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Far plane","s":"The distance beyond which the camera draws nothing. Some renderers place it at infinity.","u":"glossary.html#far-plane","w":"far clip plane far clipping plane zfar draw distance"},
  {"k":"term","t":"FBX","s":"Autodesk's 3D exchange format for meshes, materials, skeletons, animation, cameras and lights. The file format is undocumented, so…","u":"glossary.html#fbx","w":".fbx"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Feature stack","s":"The set of large rendering systems a game or engine is built around, such as Nanite, Lumen, virtual shadow maps and TSR in Unreal Engine…","u":"glossary.html#feature-stack","w":"rendering feature stack UE5 feature set Nanite, Lumen, VSM, TSR"},
  {"k":"term","t":"File locking","s":"Letting only one person change a file at a time. Used for binary files such as models and textures, where two people's edits cannot be…","u":"glossary.html#file-locking","w":"exclusive checkout lock Smart Locks checkout"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fill rate","s":"How many pixels per second a GPU's output stage can depth-test, blend and write. A pass whose cost follows the number of pixels and layers…","u":"glossary.html#fill-rate","w":"pixel fill rate fill-rate-bound fill-bound ROP-bound pixel rate"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fill rule","s":"The rule that decides which triangle owns a pixel whose centre lies exactly on an edge two triangles share. Direct3D and OpenGL use the…","u":"glossary.html#fill-rule","w":"top-left rule rasterization rules"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Film grain","s":"Fine noise added fresh every frame to imitate photographic film. It also breaks up colour banding.","u":"glossary.html#film-grain","w":"grain Grain film noise"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Filmic tone mapping","s":"Tone curves shaped like photographic film's response, with a soft toe and a long shoulder, so bright areas keep detail and the middle…","u":"glossary.html#filmic-tone-mapping","w":"filmic filmic tonemapper Filmic"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fireflies","s":"Isolated, very bright pixels from rare paths that find a small, intense light, often by way of a mirror or glossy surface. They need huge…","u":"glossary.html#fireflies","w":"firefly hot pixels Max Path Intensity Maximum Intensity"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fixed-function stage","s":"A pipeline stage built into the GPU as dedicated circuitry, such as the rasterizer or the depth test. It cannot be programmed, only…","u":"glossary.html#fixed-function-stage","w":"fixed function fixed-function hardware"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fixed hardware","s":"A platform where every player has the same machine, such as one console model, so a game can be tuned and tested for exactly that hardware.","u":"glossary.html#fixed-hardware","w":"fixed platform closed hardware"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fixed timestep","s":"Advancing a simulation, usually physics, in steps of a constant length such as 1/60 of a second, whatever the frame rate. Each rendered…","u":"glossary.html#fixed-timestep","w":"fixed update physics tick FixedUpdate() _physics_process()"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Flame chart","s":"A thread's function calls drawn along a time axis, each call as a bar under the call that made it, so a bar's width is how long that call…","u":"glossary.html#flame-chart","w":"timeline view call timeline icicle chart Timing Insights"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Flipbook","s":"An animation stored as a grid of frames in one texture. A particle shows one cell at a time and steps through them as it ages, so smoke…","u":"glossary.html#flipbook","w":"flipbook animation sprite sheet animation SubUV Sub UV Animation Texture Sheet Animation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Flow map","s":"A texture whose colours store a direction and speed for each point, used to push a material's texture coordinates so water, lava or clouds…","u":"glossary.html#flow-map","w":"flowmap flow texture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Forward+","s":"Forward rendering with per-tile or per-cluster light lists, built after a depth pre-pass, so each pixel loops only over nearby lights. It…","u":"glossary.html#forward-plus","w":"Forward Plus tiled forward rendering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Forward rendering","s":"A way of organising a frame where each object is lit as it is drawn. Simple and good for transparency and mobile GPUs.","u":"glossary.html#forward-rendering","w":"forward shading"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Field of view","s":"How wide the camera sees, as an angle across either the height or the width of the screen. Engines differ on which of the two the number…","u":"glossary.html#fov","w":"FOV view angle"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frames per second","s":"How many finished images a game shows each second. At 60 fps a new image appears about every 16.7 milliseconds.","u":"glossary.html#fps","w":"fps frame rate framerate"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fragment","s":"One triangle's contribution to one pixel: the covered sample with its interpolated values, before the depth test and blending decide…","u":"glossary.html#fragment","w":"pixel candidate"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame","s":"One complete image drawn by the game. At 60 frames per second each frame has about 16.7 milliseconds.","u":"glossary.html#frame","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame budget","s":"The time available to produce one frame at the target frame rate, split between systems: 16.7 ms at 60 fps, 11.1 ms at 90 fps, 33.3 ms at…","u":"glossary.html#frame-budget","w":"budget"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame capture","s":"A recording of every graphics API call in one frame, with the buffers and textures they used, that a tool can replay and inspect later,…","u":"glossary.html#frame-capture","w":"GPU capture GPU trace graphics capture .rdc .wpix .gputrace"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame debugger","s":"A tool that steps through a captured frame draw by draw, showing the render targets, textures, shaders and pipeline state at each step, to…","u":"glossary.html#frame-debugger","w":"graphics debugger GPU debugger Frame Debugger Graphics Capture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame generation","s":"Creating extra frames between rendered ones from the two frames around them, their motion vectors and depth, to raise the displayed frame…","u":"glossary.html#frame-generation","w":"frame gen FG DLSS Frame Generation Multi Frame Generation MFG FSR Frame Generation XeSS Frame Generation frame interpolation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame graph","s":"A description of a frame's passes and the render targets each reads and writes, from which the engine works out the order, skips unused…","u":"glossary.html#frame-graph","w":"render graph RDG Render Dependency Graph"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame latency","s":"The time from a player's input to the first image on screen that shows its effect. Every stage a frame passes through, and every frame it…","u":"glossary.html#frame-latency","w":"input latency input lag input-to-photon latency click-to-photon latency"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame pacing","s":"How evenly frames reach the screen. Two runs with the same average frame rate can feel very different if one shows its frames at uneven…","u":"glossary.html#frame-pacing","w":"frame time consistency judder microstutter Swappy"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame time","s":"How long one frame takes, in milliseconds: 1000 divided by the frame rate. Unlike frame rates, the times of separate pieces of work add…","u":"glossary.html#frame-time","w":"frametime milliseconds per frame delta time"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame-time graph","s":"A chart of how long each frame took, one bar or point per frame in order, with the budget drawn as a line. It shows the hitches and uneven…","u":"glossary.html#frame-time-graph","w":"frametime graph frame time plot stat UnitGraph"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frame timeline","s":"A frame's passes drawn as bars along time, to scale, showing where the milliseconds go, what runs at the same time and how much of the…","u":"glossary.html#frame-timeline","w":"GPU timeline timing view frame breakdown"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Framebuffer","s":"A block of GPU memory holding one whole image, one colour value per pixel. The display reads its picture from one framebuffer while the…","u":"glossary.html#framebuffer","w":"frame buffer colour buffer render target"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Framebuffer fetch","s":"A pixel shader reading the colour or depth already stored at its own pixel in the render target, without a separate pass. On tile-based…","u":"glossary.html#framebuffer-fetch","w":"programmable blending pixel local storage PLS subpassLoad dynamic rendering local read SetInputAttachment"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fresnel effect","s":"The rise in reflection as a surface is seen closer to edge-on. Water reflects about 2% of light looking straight down and nearly all of it…","u":"glossary.html#fresnel","w":"Fresnel Schlick's approximation Schlick Fresnel"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Froxel","s":"One cell of a 3D grid aligned with the camera's view: a screen tile in width and height and a depth slice deep, with slices getting deeper…","u":"glossary.html#froxel","w":"frustum voxel froxels froxel grid froxel buffer volumetric fog grid"},
  {"k":"term","t":"View frustum","s":"The shape of the space a camera can see: a pyramid with its tip cut off, bounded by the near plane, the far plane and four sides set by…","u":"glossary.html#frustum","w":"frustum camera frustum"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Frustum culling","s":"Skipping objects whose bounding volume lies entirely outside the camera's view frustum. It is cheap and every engine does it automatically.","u":"glossary.html#frustum-culling","w":"view frustum culling view culling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"FSR","s":"AMD's upscaling family: FSR 1 is spatial, FSR 2 and 3 are hand-written temporal upscalers for most GPUs, and FSR Upscaling 4 is…","u":"glossary.html#fsr","w":"AMD FidelityFX Super Resolution FSR Upscaling FSR 4 FSR 3.1 FSR 2 FSR Redstone"},
  {"k":"term","t":"FXAA","s":"Timothy Lottes's post-process anti-aliasing from NVIDIA: one full-screen pass over the tone-mapped image that finds high-contrast edges…","u":"glossary.html#fxaa","w":"Fast Approximate Anti-Aliasing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"G-buffer","s":"A set of screen-sized images holding each pixel's surface data, such as colour, normal, roughness and depth, written before lighting in…","u":"glossary.html#g-buffer","w":"geometry buffer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"G-buffer layout","s":"Which values a deferred renderer stores in its G-buffer, in which render targets and formats, and so how many bytes each pixel costs.…","u":"glossary.html#g-buffer-layout","w":"G-buffer format G-buffer packing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Game engine","s":"The reusable software a game is built on: runtime systems such as rendering, physics, animation and audio that run every frame, plus the…","u":"glossary.html#game-engine","w":"engine"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Game loop","s":"The loop at the heart of every game that, once per frame, reads input, updates the game world, draws the new image and hands it to the…","u":"glossary.html#game-loop","w":"main loop frame loop"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Game thread","s":"The CPU thread that runs the game loop: it reads input, runs gameplay code and updates the world. Most engines also call it the main thread.","u":"glossary.html#game-thread","w":"main thread GameThread"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GameObject","s":"Unity's object: a container for components that always has a Transform. Everything placed in a Unity scene is a GameObject.","u":"glossary.html#gameobject","w":"game object"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Gamma","s":"The curve that relates stored numbers to light output, close to light = value to the power 2.2. Values in gamma space look evenly spaced…","u":"glossary.html#gamma","w":"gamma encoding gamma correction gamma space transfer function"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Garbage collection","s":"Reclaiming memory automatically: the runtime finds objects nothing refers to any more and frees them. Unity's C# scripts and Unreal's…","u":"glossary.html#garbage-collection","w":"GC garbage collector managed memory GC.Collect"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GC spike","s":"A long frame caused by the garbage collector stopping a thread to do its work at once. It returns every few seconds in a game that…","u":"glossary.html#gc-spike","w":"garbage collection spike GC pause GC hitch stop-the-world pause"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Generated frame","s":"A frame made by frame generation instead of the renderer. No input is read for it, and it can only be shown once the next rendered frame…","u":"glossary.html#generated-frame","w":"interpolated frame intermediate frame fake frame"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Geometry-bound","s":"A GPU pass limited by vertex shading, triangle setup or the rasteriser, such as a shadow pass over a dense scene. Lowering the resolution…","u":"glossary.html#geometry-bound","w":"vertex-bound triangle-bound primitive-bound"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GGX","s":"The formula almost every real-time renderer uses for how a surface's microfacets are tilted at a given roughness. Its long, soft tail…","u":"glossary.html#ggx","w":"Trowbridge-Reitz GGX distribution normal distribution function NDF"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ghosting","s":"Faint trails behind moving objects, left when a temporal technique blends in history from a place the object has already left.","u":"glossary.html#ghosting","w":"smearing trails"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Git LFS","s":"An extension to Git that keeps large files on a separate server and only small pointer files in the repository. It can also lock files…","u":"glossary.html#git-lfs","w":"Git Large File Storage LFS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Global illumination","s":"Lighting that includes light bouncing between surfaces, not only light arriving straight from sources.","u":"glossary.html#global-illumination","w":"GI indirect lighting"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GLSL","s":"The Khronos Group's C-like shader language for OpenGL, OpenGL ES and WebGL, also usable for Vulkan through SPIR-V. Godot's shading…","u":"glossary.html#glsl","w":"OpenGL Shading Language GLSL ES"},
  {"k":"term","t":"glTF","s":"An open 3D delivery format from the Khronos Group, standardised as ISO/IEC 12113:2022. It stores meshes, PBR materials, skins and…","u":"glossary.html#gltf","w":"glTF 2.0 GL Transmission Format .gltf .glb"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Godot","s":"A free, open-source engine under the MIT licence, scripted mainly in its own language, GDScript, with three renderers: Forward+, Mobile…","u":"glossary.html#godot","w":"Godot Engine Godot 4 GDScript"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Node","s":"Godot's building block. Each node does one job, such as drawing a mesh or casting light, and a small tree of nodes makes an object.","u":"glossary.html#godot-node","w":"Node3D Node2D Godot node"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Godot Profiler","s":"The Profiler tab of Godot's Debugger panel. It records frame, physics and script time per function while the game runs from the editor.","u":"glossary.html#godot-profiler","w":"Profiler tab Debugger profiler"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Visual Profiler","s":"A tab of Godot's Debugger panel that shows how long each rendering step took on the CPU and on the GPU, frame by frame.","u":"glossary.html#godot-visual-profiler","w":"Godot Visual Profiler"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Gold master","s":"The final release build, approved by the platform holders and locked, that is used to manufacture discs and seed digital stores.","u":"glossary.html#gold-master","w":"gold going gold release build"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU","s":"The graphics processor: thousands of simple cores running the same small program on many vertices or pixels at once.","u":"glossary.html#gpu","w":"graphics processing unit graphics card"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU-bound","s":"A frame whose time is set by the GPU while the CPU threads wait for it. Less GPU work, such as a lower resolution or cheaper effects,…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-bound","w":"GPU bound GPU-limited"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU counters","s":"Measurements the GPU keeps for each of its units during a frame, such as how busy the shader cores, caches and memory were. Vendor and…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-counters","w":"hardware counters performance counters GPU metrics perf counters"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU culling","s":"Culling done by compute shaders on the GPU, testing every instance or cluster against the frustum and a depth pyramid and writing the…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-culling","w":"GPU occlusion culling compute culling GPU-driven culling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU decompression","s":"Unpacking compressed game data with compute shaders on the graphics card, as DirectStorage can do with GDeflate, so the CPU does not have…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-decompression","w":"GDeflate Zstandard GPU decompression hardware decompression"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU-driven rendering","s":"A way of rendering where the scene's object data lives on the GPU, compute shaders cull it and write the draw lists, and indirect draws…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-driven-rendering","w":"GPU-driven pipeline GPU Resident Drawer GPU Scene"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU occupancy","s":"How many warps a compute unit has loaded compared with the most it could hold. Low occupancy leaves fewer warps to switch to while others…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-occupancy","w":"occupancy wave occupancy warp occupancy waves in flight"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU particles","s":"Particles spawned and moved by compute shaders on the GPU instead of by the CPU. They allow hundreds of thousands to millions of…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-particles","w":"GPU simulation GPUComputeSim GPU Compute Sim VFX Graph GPUParticles3D"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU profiler","s":"A tool that measures how long each pass and draw took on the GPU and, with hardware counters, which part of the GPU held it back.","u":"glossary.html#gpu-profiler","w":"GPU timing GPU trace GPU Usage module"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU queue","s":"The line of submitted command buffers waiting for the GPU. The CPU submits a frame's work and moves on; the GPU runs it in order when it…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-queue","w":"command queue render queue frames in flight submission"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU skinning","s":"Bending an animated character's mesh on the GPU: each vertex is moved by a weighted blend of its bones' transforms, either in the vertex…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-skinning","w":"vertex shader skinning compute skinning skin cache"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU timestamp query","s":"A command in the GPU's command stream that records the GPU clock once all earlier work has finished. Two of them around a pass give its…","u":"glossary.html#gpu-timestamp-query","w":"timestamp query GPU timer query D3D12_QUERY_TYPE_TIMESTAMP vkCmdWriteTimestamp timestamp-query"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Graphics API","s":"The published set of commands a program uses to drive a GPU, such as DirectX 12, Vulkan, Metal or WebGPU. Engines reach the GPU only…","u":"glossary.html#graphics-api","w":"API rendering API"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Graphics mode","s":"A console game's menu choice between preset trade-offs, such as a Performance mode at 60 fps with a lower internal resolution and a…","u":"glossary.html#graphics-mode","w":"Performance mode Quality mode Fidelity mode Resolution mode 120 FPS mode"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Graphics pipeline","s":"The fixed sequence of stages a GPU puts every draw through: fetch the vertices, run the vertex shader, assemble triangles, find the pixels…","u":"glossary.html#graphics-pipeline","w":"GPU pipeline rendering pipeline pipeline"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Graphics programmer","s":"A programmer who builds and optimises the renderer: lighting, shadows, the material system, post-processing and the code that drives the…","u":"glossary.html#graphics-programmer","w":"rendering programmer rendering engineer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Graphics settings menu","s":"The in-game screen where players trade image quality for frame rate and memory: a preset, resolution and upscaling, and options such as…","u":"glossary.html#graphics-settings-menu","w":"video settings graphics options options menu display settings"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Green channel convention","s":"Which way green points in a normal map: up the texture in the OpenGL or Y+ convention, down in the DirectX or Y− convention. A map read…","u":"glossary.html#green-channel","w":"OpenGL normal map DirectX normal map Y+ Y- Flip Green Channel Normal Map Invert Y"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GTAO","s":"A screen-space AO method that finds the two horizons in a few slices around each pixel and integrates the visible sky between them…","u":"glossary.html#gtao","w":"ground-truth ambient occlusion XeGTAO"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Hair cards","s":"Hair drawn as textured strips, each carrying an image of many hairs with alpha. Cheap and supported everywhere, but the strips must be…","u":"glossary.html#hair-cards","w":"hair strips card-based hair"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Hair strands","s":"Hair drawn as tens of thousands of individual thin curves, each with its own shading and motion. Accurate up close but heavy, so engines…","u":"glossary.html#hair-strands","w":"strand-based hair groom Groom strands"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Half vector","s":"The unit vector half-way between the direction to the light and the direction to the camera, normalize(L + V). Where a surface's normal…","u":"glossary.html#half-vector","w":"halfway vector H"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Halton sequence","s":"A sequence of points that fills a square evenly, each new point landing in the biggest gap left by the earlier ones. Temporal…","u":"glossary.html#halton-sequence","w":"Halton (2, 3) Halton[2,3] low-discrepancy sequence"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Handedness","s":"Whether a coordinate system's x, y and z axes match the thumb, index and middle finger of a left hand or a right hand. With x to the right…","u":"glossary.html#handedness","w":"left-handed right-handed chirality"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Hard and soft edges","s":"A per-edge shading choice. Across a soft edge the normals are shared and shading blends smoothly; across a hard edge each side keeps its…","u":"glossary.html#hard-soft-edges","w":"smoothing groups sharp edges smoothing angle auto smooth"},
  {"k":"term","t":"HBAO","s":"A screen-space AO method that marches outward from each pixel in a few directions and records how high the surrounding surfaces rise above…","u":"glossary.html#hbao","w":"horizon-based ambient occlusion HBAO+"},
  {"k":"term","t":"HDR display","s":"A screen and signal that can show far brighter highlights and deeper colours than a standard (SDR) screen. The game must send a different…","u":"glossary.html#hdr-display","w":"HDR output display HDR HDR screen"},
  {"k":"term","t":"HDR rendering","s":"Lighting a frame with floating-point values that can rise far above 1.0, as real light does, and converting to the display's range only at…","u":"glossary.html#hdr-rendering","w":"scene-referred rendering high dynamic range rendering floating-point render target"},
  {"k":"term","t":"HDR10","s":"The most common HDR signal: 10-bit values on the PQ curve (SMPTE ST 2084), which encodes absolute brightness up to 10,000 nits, with Rec.…","u":"glossary.html#hdr10","w":"PQ SMPTE ST 2084 perceptual quantizer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"HDRP","s":"Unity's render pipeline for high-fidelity rendering on desktop, Xbox and PlayStation, with physical light units and advanced materials.…","u":"glossary.html#hdrp","w":"High Definition Render Pipeline"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Headroom","s":"The part of the frame budget left unused on purpose in typical scenes, often about 10%, so spikes such as explosions, streaming or the…","u":"glossary.html#headroom","w":"frame headroom budget headroom safety margin spare time"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Height fog","s":"Fog whose density falls exponentially with height, so it pools near the ground and in valleys and thins towards the sky. The fog along…","u":"glossary.html#height-fog","w":"exponential height fog Exponential Height Fog height-based fog ground fog"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Height map","s":"A greyscale texture where brightness means height above the surface. Used to make normal maps, for parallax effects and to displace real…","u":"glossary.html#height-map","w":"heightmap displacement map depth map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Hi-Z occlusion culling","s":"Occlusion culling against a depth pyramid: a chain of ever smaller copies of the depth buffer in which each texel keeps the farthest depth…","u":"glossary.html#hi-z","w":"Hi-Z HZB hierarchical Z-buffer depth pyramid hierarchical depth"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Hi-Z tracing","s":"Ray marching through a depth pyramid whose texels keep the nearest depth below them. While a ray stays in front of a cell's nearest…","u":"glossary.html#hi-z-tracing","w":"Hi-Z screen-space tracing HZB traversal hierarchical ray marching hierarchical depth tracing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"History buffer","s":"The image in which a temporal technique keeps its result from the previous frame. The next frame reprojects it and blends in its new…","u":"glossary.html#history-buffer","w":"TAA history accumulation buffer previous frame buffer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"History rejection","s":"Discarding or down-weighting a pixel's history when it is probably wrong, for example because depth or speed changed sharply or the pixel…","u":"glossary.html#history-rejection","w":"history invalidation Speed rejection disocclusion detection"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Hitch","s":"A frame, or a short run of frames, that takes far longer than the frames around it, so motion visibly freezes or jumps even when the…","u":"glossary.html#hitch","w":"stutter frame spike spike"},
  {"k":"term","t":"HLOD","s":"Replacing a whole group of distant objects, such as a city block, with one merged and simplified mesh and material, built offline, so the…","u":"glossary.html#hlod","w":"hierarchical level of detail proxy mesh visibility ranges"},
  {"k":"term","t":"HLSL","s":"Microsoft's C-like language for writing shaders, used by Direct3D and by Unreal and Unity for every platform. Compilers translate it to…","u":"glossary.html#hlsl","w":"High-Level Shader Language High-Level Shading Language .usf"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Horizon angle","s":"In one direction from a point, the steepest angle at which surrounding geometry rises above it. Everything below that angle is blocked;…","u":"glossary.html#horizon-angle","w":"horizon"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Human review gate","s":"A step where a named person must inspect and approve AI-made work before it is merged, shipped or built on. It covers what automated…","u":"glossary.html#human-review-gate","w":"review gate human in the loop sign-off approval step"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Hybrid rendering","s":"Drawing what the camera sees with rasterization and tracing rays only for effects such as shadows, reflections and bounced light, starting…","u":"glossary.html#hybrid-rendering","w":"hybrid ray tracing hybrid renderer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"IES light profile","s":"A file from a lamp maker that lists how strong a fixture is in each direction, in the Illuminating Engineering Society's LM-63 format.…","u":"glossary.html#ies-profile","w":"IES profile IES file IES Texture photometric profile LM-63"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Image-based lighting","s":"Lighting surfaces from an environment map, treating every texel as light arriving from its direction, instead of only from individual…","u":"glossary.html#image-based-lighting","w":"IBL environment lighting"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Immediate-mode GPU","s":"A GPU that draws triangles in the order they arrive and writes each pixel's colour and depth straight to full-screen buffers in memory.…","u":"glossary.html#immediate-mode-gpu","w":"immediate-mode rendering IMR"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Importance sampling","s":"Choosing random directions in proportion to how much light they are likely to bring, for example aiming at lights or along a shiny…","u":"glossary.html#importance-sampling","w":"light sampling next event estimation NEE BRDF sampling multiple importance sampling MIS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Impostor","s":"A picture of an object, rendered ahead of time from one or more directions, drawn on a flat two-triangle card in its place once the object…","u":"glossary.html#impostor","w":"imposter impostor card octahedral impostor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"In-scattering","s":"Light from the sun, the sky or a lamp that particles along a view ray scatter towards the camera. It is what makes fog, haze and light…","u":"glossary.html#in-scattering","w":"inscattering fog inscattering single scattering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Incremental garbage collection","s":"Splitting the garbage collector's work into small slices spread over several frames, so no single frame pays for it all. It does not…","u":"glossary.html#incremental-gc","w":"incremental GC Use Incremental GC incremental reachability gc.AllowIncrementalReachability"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Index buffer","s":"A GPU buffer listing which vertices form each triangle, by row number in the vertex buffer. It lets triangles share corners instead of…","u":"glossary.html#index-buffer","w":"IBO element buffer element array"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Index format","s":"How many bytes each index uses. 16-bit indices are half the size but can only address 65,536 vertices; bigger meshes need 32-bit indices…","u":"glossary.html#index-format","w":"16-bit indices 32-bit indices UInt16 UInt32"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Indirect drawing","s":"A draw call whose numbers, such as how many indices and instances to draw, are read from a buffer in GPU memory instead of being supplied…","u":"glossary.html#indirect-drawing","w":"indirect draw DrawIndirect ExecuteIndirect multi-draw indirect MDI"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Indirect light","s":"Light that reaches a surface after bouncing off at least one other surface. It is why shadows are rarely pure black.","u":"glossary.html#indirect-light","w":"bounce light indirect lighting"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Inline ray tracing","s":"Tracing a ray from inside any shader, such as a compute or pixel shader, and handling the result in the same code, without separate hit…","u":"glossary.html#inline-ray-tracing","w":"ray queries ray query RayQuery TraceRayInline"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Input assembler","s":"The fixed GPU stage that reads the index buffer, fetches the vertex rows it points to and groups them into triangles for the vertex shader.","u":"glossary.html#input-assembler","w":"input assembly vertex fetch"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Override","s":"A value changed on one prefab instance only. It always wins over the prefab's own value, so later edits to that value on the prefab do not…","u":"glossary.html#instance-override","w":"instance override prefab override"},
  {"k":"term","t":"GPU instancing","s":"Drawing many copies of the same mesh in one draw call, each with its own position and small per-copy data.","u":"glossary.html#instancing","w":"instancing instanced drawing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Interleaved layout","s":"Storing all of a vertex's attributes next to each other in one row, then the next vertex's row, and so on.","u":"glossary.html#interleaved","w":"array of structures AoS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Internal resolution","s":"The resolution an effect or the whole frame is actually computed at, before being scaled to the output. Half resolution means a quarter of…","u":"glossary.html#internal-resolution","w":"render resolution half resolution"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Interpolator","s":"A value the vertex shader outputs at each corner, such as a UV or a normal, which the GPU blends across the triangle so every pixel's…","u":"glossary.html#interpolator","w":"varying varyings vertex output TEXCOORD custom interpolator"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Inverse-square law","s":"The rule that light from a point spreads over an area growing with the square of the distance, so twice as far gets a quarter of the…","u":"glossary.html#inverse-square-law","w":"inverse-square falloff inverse squared falloff Use Inverse Squared Falloff"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Irradiance","s":"All the light arriving at a point on a surface from every direction above it, each weighted by how squarely it strikes. It decides how…","u":"glossary.html#irradiance","w":"E incident light"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Irradiance map","s":"A version of an environment map storing, for each direction a surface could face, all the light arriving over its half-sphere, weighted by…","u":"glossary.html#irradiance-map","w":"irradiance environment map diffuse convolution diffuse environment map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Irradiance volume","s":"Light probes arranged in a 3D grid that fills the level, often denser near geometry, so any point in the air can look up baked indirect…","u":"glossary.html#irradiance-volume","w":"probe grid probe volume volumetric lightmap irradiance field"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Job system","s":"An engine service that splits work into small independent jobs and runs them on a pool of worker threads, about one per CPU core, so…","u":"glossary.html#job-system","w":"worker threads task system task graph WorkerThreadPool"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lambertian diffuse","s":"The simplest diffuse model: the surface sends the light it receives equally in all directions, so its brightness depends only on how…","u":"glossary.html#lambert","w":"Lambert Lambert diffuse Lambertian"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Latency hiding","s":"How a GPU keeps working while memory requests are in flight: when one warp stops to wait for data, the compute unit switches at no cost to…","u":"glossary.html#latency-hiding","w":"hiding memory latency warp switching"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lens distortion","s":"Bending of straight lines in the image, outwards (barrel) or inwards (pincushion), as wide or cheap lenses do; applied as a final warp of…","u":"glossary.html#lens-distortion","w":"barrel distortion pincushion distortion"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lens flare","s":"Ghost images and streaks of a bright light, as reflections between a lens's glass elements would make, drawn along a line through the…","u":"glossary.html#lens-flare","w":"lens flares flare ghosts Screen Space Lens Flare Lens Flare (SRP)"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Level streaming","s":"Loading and unloading whole pieces of the game world, with their objects, while the game runs, so only the part near the player is in…","u":"glossary.html#level-streaming","w":"world streaming sublevel streaming additive scene loading"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light attenuation","s":"How a light weakens with distance from its source. Physically based engines use the inverse-square law, faded smoothly to zero at the…","u":"glossary.html#light-attenuation","w":"falloff light falloff attenuation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light culling","s":"Working out ahead of shading which lights can reach which part of the screen or which objects, so each pixel only evaluates the lights…","u":"glossary.html#light-culling","w":"light assignment light binning light lists"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light leaking","s":"Light appearing where it cannot reach, typically inside a room through a wall, because the GI method stores light more coarsely than the…","u":"glossary.html#light-leaking","w":"light leaks leaks dark leaking shadow leaking"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light mobility","s":"A per-light setting that decides which of its lighting is baked and which is computed every frame. Unreal calls the choices Static,…","u":"glossary.html#light-mobility","w":"Mobility Light Mode Bake Mode Static Stationary Movable Baked Mixed Realtime"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light probe","s":"A point in empty space where the bake records the light arriving from every direction, usually as a few spherical harmonics numbers.…","u":"glossary.html#light-probe","w":"Light Probe Group LightmapProbe irradiance probe probe"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light propagation volumes","s":"A real-time GI method from Crytek that injects light from lit surfaces into a coarse 3D grid of spherical harmonics, then spreads it cell…","u":"glossary.html#light-propagation-volumes","w":"LPV cascaded light propagation volumes CLPV"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light shafts","s":"Visible beams of light in hazy air, with darker air where objects cast shadows into it. Rendered by shadowed volumetric fog, or more…","u":"glossary.html#light-shafts","w":"god rays crepuscular rays sun shafts volumetric shadows light beams"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light-space depth","s":"A point's distance from a light measured along the light's direction, in the shadow map's own coordinates. The shadow test compares it…","u":"glossary.html#light-space-depth","w":"light depth depth from the light"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Light volume","s":"In classic deferred rendering, a simple shape drawn for each light, such as a sphere for a point light, so that only the pixels inside the…","u":"glossary.html#light-volume","w":"deferred light volume stencil light volume"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lighting artist","s":"An artist who places and tunes lights, sky, fog and exposure in a level to create mood and make it readable, within the lighting budget.","u":"glossary.html#lighting-artist","w":"lighter"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lightmap","s":"A texture holding precomputed lighting for static surfaces, laid out with its own UV coordinates.","u":"glossary.html#lightmap","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lightmap resolution","s":"How many lightmap texels cover each metre of a surface. Doubling it sharpens baked shadows and quadruples the texels, so memory and bake…","u":"glossary.html#lightmap-resolution","w":"lightmap texel density Light Map Resolution texels per unit Lightmap Texel Size Scale In Lightmap"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lightmap seam","s":"A visible line in baked lighting where two pieces of a lightmap UV layout meet on the model but lie apart in the texture, so their texels…","u":"glossary.html#lightmap-seam","w":"lighting seam seam artefact light bleed padding bleed"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lightmap UVs","s":"A second set of texture coordinates in which every face of a mesh has its own space in the lightmap, with no overlaps and a gap of a few…","u":"glossary.html#lightmap-uvs","w":"lightmap UV channel second UV channel UV2 UV1 Mesh.uv2 Lightmap Coordinate Index"},
  {"k":"term","t":"LightmapGI","s":"Godot's node for baked global illumination. It bakes lightmaps on the GPU in the editor and can generate light probes so moving objects…","u":"glossary.html#lightmapgi","w":"LightmapGI node Godot lightmapper"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lightmapper","s":"The editor tool that computes baked lighting by tracing many light paths from every lightmap texel and probe, then writes the results to…","u":"glossary.html#lightmapper","w":"light baker bake lighting build Lightmass GPU Lightmass Progressive Lightmapper Unity Compute Light Baker"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Linear blend skinning","s":"The standard skinning method: move a vertex with each of its bones, then average the results by its weights. Fast, but joints lose…","u":"glossary.html#linear-blend-skinning","w":"LBS smooth skinning matrix palette skinning"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Linear colour","s":"Colour values proportional to the amount of light, so twice the number means twice the light. Lighting, blending and blurring only give…","u":"glossary.html#linear-colour","w":"linear space linear light scene-linear"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Live ops","s":"The ongoing work of running a released game: hotfixes, scheduled patches, events, seasons and new content, each one built, tested and…","u":"glossary.html#live-ops","w":"live operations LiveOps games as a service"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Load action","s":"What a render pass does with an image when it starts: load its old contents from memory, clear it to a value, or start with whatever is…","u":"glossary.html#load-action","w":"load op loadOp VK_ATTACHMENT_LOAD_OP MTLLoadAction Load, Clear, Don't care"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Loading range","s":"The distance from the player, or another streaming source, within which a streaming system keeps the world loaded. A longer range gives…","u":"glossary.html#loading-range","w":"streaming distance streaming radius Loading Range"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Local transform","s":"An object's position, rotation and scale measured from its parent. It stays the same when the parent moves.","u":"glossary.html#local-transform","w":"relative transform localPosition relative location"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Level of detail","s":"Simpler versions of a mesh swapped in as it gets smaller on screen, so distant objects cost less to draw.","u":"glossary.html#lod","w":"LOD"},
  {"k":"term","t":"LOD bias","s":"A number added to the mip level the GPU would choose. Negative values pick larger, sharper levels at the risk of shimmer; positive values…","u":"glossary.html#lod-bias","w":"mip bias mipmap bias MipLODBias texture mipmap bias"},
  {"k":"term","t":"LOD cross-fade","s":"Hiding a level-of-detail switch by drawing both levels for a short range or time and handing pixels from one to the other, usually through…","u":"glossary.html#lod-cross-fade","w":"dithered LOD transition LOD dither cross fade LOD fade"},
  {"k":"term","t":"LOD popping","s":"The visible jump when an object switches from one level of detail to the next in a single frame, seen as a sudden change of outline or…","u":"glossary.html#lod-popping","w":"popping LOD pop"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Look development","s":"The work of finding and proving a game's visual style inside the engine, through target images, test scenes, materials and lighting, until…","u":"glossary.html#look-development","w":"look dev lookdev"},
  {"k":"term","t":"LTS","s":"An engine release that keeps getting bug fixes for an extended period instead of new features. Teams in full production usually stay on one.","u":"glossary.html#lts","w":"long-term support"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lumen","s":"Unreal Engine 5's real-time global illumination and reflection system. It computes bounced light, and short-range occlusion, without baking.","u":"glossary.html#lumen","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lumen Lite","s":"Unreal Engine 5.8's medium-quality Lumen, in Beta: global illumination from a field of irradiance probes and screen-space reflections…","u":"glossary.html#lumen-lite","w":"Lumen Medium Quality Irradiance Field Gather r.Lumen.FinalGatherMethod 0"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lumen (unit)","s":"The unit of all the visible light a source gives out, in every direction. A candle gives about 13 lumens and a household bulb 600 to 1,200.","u":"glossary.html#lumen-unit","w":"lm lumens luminous flux"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Luminance histogram","s":"A count of the frame's pixels in brightness bands on a log scale. Auto-exposure uses it to ignore the darkest and brightest pixels before…","u":"glossary.html#luminance-histogram","w":"exposure histogram histogram metering Automatic Histogram"},
  {"k":"term","t":"LUT","s":"A small 3D texture, typically 16 or 32 entries along each side, that maps every input colour to an output colour, so a whole grade costs…","u":"glossary.html#lut","w":"lookup table 3D LUT colour lookup table Color Lookup Color Grading LUT lookup texture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Lux","s":"The unit of light arriving on a surface: one lumen per square metre. Direct sunlight gives tens of thousands of lux, a full moon well…","u":"glossary.html#lux","w":"lx illuminance lumens per square metre"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Magnitude","s":"The length of a vector, found with Pythagoras: the square root of the sum of its squared components. The vector (3, 4) has a magnitude of 5.","u":"glossary.html#magnitude","w":"length vector length norm size"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Manifold","s":"A mesh whose surface could be the skin of a real object, with every edge shared by at most two faces. Non-manifold geometry, such as three…","u":"glossary.html#manifold","w":"watertight non-manifold closed mesh"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mass Entity","s":"Unreal Engine's data-oriented framework for large numbers of similar things, such as crowds and traffic. Its components are called…","u":"glossary.html#mass-entity","w":"Mass MassEntity Mass AI"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Material","s":"The description of how a surface treats light: a shader plus the textures and values that feed it, such as base colour, metallic and…","u":"glossary.html#material","w":"material asset StandardMaterial3D Lit material"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Material instance","s":"A copy of a parent material that reuses its compiled shader with different parameter values, such as another tint or texture. Instances…","u":"glossary.html#material-instance","w":"Material Instance Material Variant material override MIC MID"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Material sorting","s":"Ordering a pass's draw calls so that draws sharing a shader, then a material, then a mesh run one after another, which cuts the number of…","u":"glossary.html#material-sorting","w":"state sorting draw sorting sort key"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Matrix","s":"A grid of numbers, usually 4 by 4 in graphics, that stores a transform. Multiplying a position by it applies the transform, and…","u":"glossary.html#matrix","w":"matrices 4×4 matrix transformation matrix float4x4"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Bone influences per vertex","s":"The most bones allowed to move one vertex, commonly 4, sometimes 8 or more. Extra influences are dropped and the rest rescaled, which can…","u":"glossary.html#max-bone-influences","w":"max influences bones per vertex Max Bones/Vertex Skin Weights quality"},
  {"k":"term","t":"MegaLights","s":"Unreal Engine 5's stochastic direct lighting: each pixel traces a fixed number of rays towards the lights most likely to matter and a…","u":"glossary.html#megalights","w":"r.MegaLights MegaLights Shadow Method"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Memory bandwidth","s":"How many bytes per second a memory can deliver to the processors reading it. Many rendering passes are limited by it more than by…","u":"glossary.html#memory-bandwidth","w":"bandwidth GB/s"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Memory budget","s":"How much memory a game may use on each platform, split between its systems such as render targets, textures, meshes, audio and gameplay,…","u":"glossary.html#memory-budget","w":"RAM budget memory map memory plan"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Memory fragmentation","s":"Free memory broken into gaps between objects that are each too small for a large request, so an allocation can fail while the total free…","u":"glossary.html#memory-fragmentation","w":"fragmentation heap fragmentation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Memoryless attachment","s":"A render target that exists only in tile memory for one render pass and has no copy in main memory, such as a depth buffer or MSAA samples…","u":"glossary.html#memoryless-attachment","w":"memoryless MTLStorageModeMemoryless transient attachment VK_IMAGE_USAGE_TRANSIENT_ATTACHMENT_BIT lazily allocated memory"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Merged mesh","s":"Several objects joined into one mesh asset, by hand or with a tool, so they draw as one object with one draw call per material. It is then…","u":"glossary.html#merged-mesh","w":"combined mesh mesh merging Merge Actors"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mesh","s":"The shape of an object as a set of triangles joined at shared corners, plus the per-corner data needed to draw it.","u":"glossary.html#mesh","w":"model geometry"},
  {"k":"term","t":"LOD bias (meshes)","s":"A setting that moves every level-of-detail switch point at once, so the same scene uses more or less detail. In Unity and Godot, values…","u":"glossary.html#mesh-lod-bias","w":"LOD Bias lodBias lod_bias LOD distance scale"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mesh shader","s":"A newer kind of shader that replaces the input assembler and vertex shader. Each group of threads loads a small chunk of a mesh from any…","u":"glossary.html#mesh-shader","w":"amplification shader task shader object shader mesh shading"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mesh simplification","s":"Making a version of a mesh with fewer triangles automatically, usually by collapsing edges one at a time in the order that changes the…","u":"glossary.html#mesh-simplification","w":"decimation polygon reduction mesh reduction auto LOD"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mesh streaming","s":"Loading the detailed levels of detail of meshes, or clusters of their triangles, only when the camera is close enough to need them, and…","u":"glossary.html#mesh-streaming","w":"geometry streaming LOD streaming Nanite streaming"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Meshlet","s":"A small chunk of a mesh, typically 64 to 128 triangles, that modern renderers cull and process as a unit.","u":"glossary.html#meshlet","w":"cluster"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Metal","s":"Apple's graphics API for Mac, iPhone, iPad and Vision Pro, and the only modern API native to them.","u":"glossary.html#metal","w":"Metal 4"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Metal/roughness workflow","s":"The standard way to author PBR materials, with base colour, metallic and roughness inputs. The shader derives the diffuse colour and F0,…","u":"glossary.html#metal-roughness","w":"metallic/roughness metalness workflow Metallic workflow"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Metallic","s":"A material input that says whether a surface is a metal (1) or not (0). Metals have no diffuse colour and tint their reflections with the…","u":"glossary.html#metallic","w":"metalness metal mask Metallic Map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Microfacets","s":"The model of a surface as countless tiny flat mirrors, each tilted slightly and far smaller than a pixel. How much they disagree in…","u":"glossary.html#microfacet","w":"microfacet model microsurface"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Middle grey","s":"A surface reflecting 18% of the light, which looks halfway between black and white. Exposure aims to show a scene's average brightness at…","u":"glossary.html#middle-grey","w":"mid-grey 18% grey key value Target Mid Gray"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mie scattering","s":"Scattering by particles about as large as the wavelength of light or larger, such as dust, pollution and water droplets. It affects all…","u":"glossary.html#mie-scattering","w":"Mie aerosol scattering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"MikkTSpace","s":"A standard way of computing a mesh's tangents, by Morten Mikkelsen, that gives the same result in every tool. When the baker and the…","u":"glossary.html#mikktspace","w":"Mikktspace Calculate Mikktspace synced tangent space"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Milestone","s":"A dated checkpoint where the team delivers a build and evidence, and the people funding the game decide whether to continue. Its criteria…","u":"glossary.html#milestone","w":"gate milestone review"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Minimum specification","s":"The least powerful hardware a PC game promises to run on, at its lowest settings. It should be a machine the team has actually tested.","u":"glossary.html#min-spec","w":"min spec minimum requirements minimum system requirements"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mip level selection","s":"How the GPU picks a mip level for each pixel: it measures how many texels the pixel spans from the UV differences between neighbouring…","u":"glossary.html#mip-level-selection","w":"texture LOD mip selection lambda"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mip streaming","s":"The common form of texture streaming: for each texture, keep in memory only the mip levels its nearest visible use needs, and load sharper…","u":"glossary.html#mip-streaming","w":"Mipmap Streaming mipmap streaming mip-level streaming"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Mipmap","s":"A chain of copies of a texture, each half the width and height of the one before, down to 1 × 1. The GPU reads the copy that best matches…","u":"glossary.html#mipmap","w":"mip chain mips mip level MIP map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Model context","s":"Everything an AI model can see while it works: the instructions, the files and results it has been given or has read, and the conversation…","u":"glossary.html#model-context","w":"context window context prompt context"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Model matrix","s":"The matrix that takes an object's vertices from object space into world space. It is built from the object's position, rotation and scale.","u":"glossary.html#model-matrix","w":"world matrix object-to-world matrix local-to-world matrix M"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Monte Carlo integration","s":"Estimating an average, such as all the light arriving at a point, from random samples. The error shrinks with the square root of the…","u":"glossary.html#monte-carlo-integration","w":"Monte Carlo random sampling stochastic sampling Monte Carlo estimator"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Motion blur","s":"Smearing each pixel along its motion vector, as a real shutter does while things move, so motion looks smoother. Camera blur comes from…","u":"glossary.html#motion-blur","w":"camera motion blur object motion blur per-object motion blur"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Motion vectors","s":"A screen-sized buffer holding, for each pixel, how far its surface moved on screen since the last frame. Temporal anti-aliasing, upscalers…","u":"glossary.html#motion-vectors","w":"velocity buffer velocities motion vector buffer MVs Output Velocity"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Multiple render targets","s":"Writing several images at once from one pixel shader, such as base colour, normals and roughness into separate render targets. Deferred…","u":"glossary.html#mrt","w":"MRT MRTs"},
  {"k":"term","t":"MSAA","s":"Multisample anti-aliasing: the GPU tests coverage and depth at several points per pixel but runs the pixel shader about once per pixel per…","u":"glossary.html#msaa","w":"multisample anti-aliasing MSAA 4x multisampling MSAA resolve"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Multi-bounce AO","s":"A correction that brightens AO on light-coloured surfaces, because light bounces several times in a bright crease before it is absorbed.","u":"glossary.html#multi-bounce-ao","w":"multi-bounce approximation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"MVP matrix","s":"The model, view and projection matrices multiplied into one, so a vertex shader can take a vertex from object space to clip space in a…","u":"glossary.html#mvp","w":"model-view-projection WorldViewProjection WVP"},
  {"k":"term","t":"N·L","s":"The dot product of the surface normal and the direction to the light, which equals the cosine of the angle between them. It measures how…","u":"glossary.html#n-dot-l","w":"N dot L NdotL NoL cosine term Lambert's cosine law"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Nanite","s":"Unreal Engine 5's geometry system. It splits meshes into clusters and streams and draws only the detail each part of the screen needs.","u":"glossary.html#nanite","w":"virtualized geometry"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Fallback mesh","s":"A simplified ordinary mesh that Unreal builds from each Nanite mesh for the places Nanite's own format is not used, most importantly the…","u":"glossary.html#nanite-fallback-mesh","w":"Nanite fallback mesh Fallback Relative Error Fallback Target"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Normalised device coordinates","s":"Clip-space positions after the divide by w. Everything visible lies between -1 and 1 across and up, and inside a fixed depth range,…","u":"glossary.html#ndc","w":"NDC normalized device coordinates"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Near plane","s":"The distance in front of the camera where drawing starts; anything closer is cut away. It has a large effect on depth precision, so it…","u":"glossary.html#near-plane","w":"near clip plane near clipping plane znear"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Nearest filtering","s":"Reading only the single texel closest to the sample point. It is the cheapest filter: magnified texels show as hard blocks and distant…","u":"glossary.html#nearest-filtering","w":"point filtering point sampling nearest-neighbour Point (no filter)"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Neighbourhood clamping","s":"Limiting a pixel's reprojected history to the range of colours in its 3 by 3 neighbourhood in the current frame, so stale history that no…","u":"glossary.html#neighbourhood-clamping","w":"neighborhood clamping history clamping colour clamping variance clipping Variance Clamp Scale"},
  {"k":"term","t":"N-gon","s":"A face with more than four corners. Modelling tools allow them, but each tool may split them into triangles differently, so they often…","u":"glossary.html#ngon","w":"ngon polygon"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Nit","s":"The unit of screen brightness: one candela per square metre. Standard screens show white at roughly 100 to 300 nits; HDR screens reach 400…","u":"glossary.html#nit","w":"nits cd/m² candela per square metre"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Sampling noise","s":"Random speckle in an effect that was computed from too few samples per pixel. More samples, or denoising, removes it.","u":"glossary.html#noise","w":"noise grain"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Normal","s":"A direction pointing straight out of a surface. Lighting uses it to work out how directly light hits that point.","u":"glossary.html#normal","w":"surface normal vertex normal"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Normal map","s":"A texture that stores a surface direction for every texel as red, green and blue. Lighting reads it per pixel, so flat triangles shade as…","u":"glossary.html#normal-map","w":"normal texture tangent-space normal map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Normal offset","s":"Moving a point a fraction of a texel along its surface normal before the shadow test, or shrinking casters along theirs, which removes…","u":"glossary.html#normal-offset","w":"normal bias normal offset shadows"},
  {"k":"term","t":"NVIDIA Nsight Graphics","s":"NVIDIA's tool for debugging and profiling Direct3D, Vulkan and OpenGL frames, with GPU Trace for unit-level profiling on NVIDIA GPUs from…","u":"glossary.html#nsight-graphics","w":"Nsight GPU Trace Graphics Capture Nsight Graphics"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Object pooling","s":"Keeping a set of frequently used objects, such as projectiles or effects, alive and reusing them instead of creating and destroying them,…","u":"glossary.html#object-pooling","w":"pooling object pool UnityEngine.Pool"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Object space","s":"Coordinates measured from a model's own origin, the way the artist built it. Vertex buffers store positions in object space.","u":"glossary.html#object-space","w":"model space local space mesh space"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Object-space normal map","s":"A normal map whose directions are relative to the whole model rather than to the surface. It needs no tangents, but works only on the mesh…","u":"glossary.html#object-space-normal-map","w":"object space normal map model-space normal map world-space normal map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Occludee","s":"An object that occlusion culling tests and may skip because occluders hide it. Small and medium objects are usually occludees only.","u":"glossary.html#occludee","w":"Occludee Static"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Occluder","s":"An object used to hide others during occlusion culling, usually a large solid one such as a wall, a building or terrain. Engines often use…","u":"glossary.html#occluder","w":"Occluder Static OccluderInstance3D"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Occlusion culling","s":"Skipping objects that are inside the view but completely hidden behind other objects, such as buildings or walls.","u":"glossary.html#occlusion-culling","w":"OC occlusion queries hardware occlusion queries dynamic occlusion"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Off-screen miss","s":"A screen-space ray that leaves the frame, or passes behind the camera, before it hits anything. The depth buffer has no data there, so a…","u":"glossary.html#off-screen-miss","w":"ray left the screen screen-edge miss"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Order-independent transparency","s":"Techniques that blend transparent layers correctly per pixel whatever order they are drawn in, either by storing and sorting every layer…","u":"glossary.html#oit","w":"OIT per-pixel sorting weighted blended OIT depth peeling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"On-tile resolve","s":"Averaging MSAA samples into one colour per pixel as each finished tile is written out, so the samples never reach main memory. It makes…","u":"glossary.html#on-tile-resolve","w":"MSAA resolve on tile inline resolve multisampleResolve resolve attachment pResolveAttachments"},
  {"k":"term","t":"1% low","s":"The frame rate of a run's slowest frames: in NVIDIA's FrameView, the average of the slowest 1% of frames, while other tools report the…","u":"glossary.html#one-percent-low","w":"1% lows 1% low fps 0.1% low 99th percentile frame time P1"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Open source","s":"Software whose source anyone may use, change and redistribute under a licence such as MIT. Godot is open source, and games made with it…","u":"glossary.html#open-source","w":"FOSS MIT licence"},
  {"k":"term","t":"OpenGL","s":"The older cross-platform graphics API, with OpenGL ES on phones and WebGL in browsers as its cut-down relatives. Still widely supported,…","u":"glossary.html#opengl","w":"OpenGL ES GLES WebGL GL"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Orthographic projection","s":"A projection in which lines of sight run parallel, so things keep their size at any distance. Used for 2D games, UI, top-down strategy…","u":"glossary.html#orthographic-projection","w":"orthographic ortho orthogonal parallel projection"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Out of memory","s":"A request for memory that cannot be met. On consoles there is no paging to fall back on, so the game usually crashes.","u":"glossary.html#out-of-memory","w":"OOM OOM crash allocation failure"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Output merger","s":"The fixed last stage of the pipeline. It tests each shaded pixel's depth against the depth buffer, then writes or blends its colour into…","u":"glossary.html#output-merger","w":"ROP render output unit raster operations OM"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Overdraw","s":"Shading the same pixel more than once in a frame because several surfaces cover it. An average overdraw of 3 means three pixel shader runs…","u":"glossary.html#overdraw","w":"overshading overdraw count"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Parallax mapping","s":"Shifting each pixel's texture lookup by an amount taken from a height map and the viewing angle, so deep detail seems to move correctly…","u":"glossary.html#parallax-mapping","w":"offset mapping Bump Offset virtual displacement mapping"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Parallelism","s":"Doing many pieces of the same job at the same time instead of one after another. GPUs are built for it: thousands of lanes each work on…","u":"glossary.html#parallelism","w":"parallel processing data parallelism"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Participating media","s":"Anything light travels through that scatters or absorbs some of it on the way, such as air, haze, fog, smoke, cloud or murky water.","u":"glossary.html#participating-media","w":"participating medium volumetric media medium"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Particle system","s":"An effect made of many small, short-lived elements, such as sparks, smoke puffs or raindrops, that an emitter spawns, moves every frame by…","u":"glossary.html#particle-system","w":"particles emitter particle effect Niagara system Particle System VFX"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pass ordering","s":"The order in which a frame's passes run. A pass must come after every pass whose output it reads; passes that do not read each other's…","u":"glossary.html#pass-ordering","w":"pass order render order frame order"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Path tracing","s":"Rendering by following random paths from the camera, bounce after bounce, until they reach light, and averaging many paths per pixel. With…","u":"glossary.html#path-tracing","w":"path tracer full ray tracing Monte Carlo path tracing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Physically based rendering","s":"Rendering in which materials state measurable properties of a surface and lights follow real physics, so the same asset looks right under…","u":"glossary.html#pbr","w":"PBR physically based shading PBS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Percentage-closer filtering","s":"Softening shadow edges by running the shadow test against several nearby texels and averaging the answers, so a pixel can be partly lit.…","u":"glossary.html#pcf","w":"PCF shadow filtering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Percentage-closer soft shadows","s":"A shadow filter that estimates how far each pixel is from its blocker and widens the PCF kernel with that distance, so shadows are sharp…","u":"glossary.html#pcss","w":"PCSS contact-hardening shadows contact hardening"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Per-frame allocation","s":"Memory a game allocates in every frame of ordinary play, such as temporary strings, lists or closures. In a garbage-collected language…","u":"glossary.html#per-frame-allocation","w":"GC.Alloc allocation churn garbage per frame GC Allocated In Frame"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Per-instance data","s":"The small row of numbers each copy gets in an instanced draw, usually its transform and perhaps a tint. Shaders read it by instance…","u":"glossary.html#per-instance-data","w":"instance buffer instance data Per Instance Custom Data"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Per-light cost","s":"The GPU time each dynamic light adds: roughly the pixels it reaches times the shading work for each, plus any shadow maps it renders. It…","u":"glossary.html#per-light-cost","w":"light count light complexity light budget"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Per-vertex lighting","s":"Running the lighting only at each vertex and blending the results across the triangle. It is cheaper than lighting every pixel, but…","u":"glossary.html#per-vertex-lighting","w":"vertex lighting Gouraud shading"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Perforce","s":"A version control system built for very large binary files and large teams, with file locking. Its server, long called Helix Core, is now…","u":"glossary.html#perforce","w":"P4 Helix Core p4"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Perspective aliasing","s":"Blocky shadows near the camera caused by a shadow map whose texels are all the same size while screen pixels cover more ground the further…","u":"glossary.html#perspective-aliasing","w":"shadow aliasing near the camera"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Perspective-correct interpolation","s":"Blending vertex values so they follow the 3D surface rather than the flat screen, by dividing by depth before blending. Without it, called…","u":"glossary.html#perspective-correct-interpolation","w":"perspective correction affine interpolation affine texture mapping"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Perspective divide","s":"The step after the vertex shader where the GPU divides a clip-space position's x, y and z by its w. With a perspective projection w grows…","u":"glossary.html#perspective-divide","w":"divide by w homogeneous divide w-divide"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Perspective projection","s":"A projection in which lines of sight meet at the camera, so distant things look smaller. The usual choice for 3D views.","u":"glossary.html#perspective-projection","w":"perspective"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Peter-panning","s":"Shadows that start some distance from the object casting them, so it seems to float. It comes from too much shadow bias or normal offset…","u":"glossary.html#peter-panning","w":"shadow detachment floating shadows"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Phase function","s":"How much light a particle scatters in each direction relative to where the light came from. Haze and fog scatter mostly forwards, so they…","u":"glossary.html#phase-function","w":"anisotropy Anisotropy Scattering Distribution Henyey-Greenstein g Mie Anisotropy"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Phong reflection model","s":"An early shading model (Bui Tuong Phong, 1975) whose highlight measures how close the light's mirror direction is to the view direction,…","u":"glossary.html#phong","w":"Phong Phong specular"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Physical light units","s":"Light intensities given in real units such as lumens, candelas, lux and nits, so engine values can be compared with real lamps, daylight…","u":"glossary.html#physical-light-units","w":"photometric units PLU real-world light units"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Physics engine","s":"The engine system that moves objects under forces such as gravity, and detects and resolves collisions between them.","u":"glossary.html#physics-engine","w":"physics Chaos PhysX Jolt"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Physics interpolation","s":"Drawing each moving object part-way between its last two physics steps, according to how far the current frame lies between them, so…","u":"glossary.html#physics-interpolation","w":"interpolation Rigidbody interpolation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pipeline stage","s":"One step of the graphics pipeline, with a fixed input and output. All stages work at the same time on different triangles and pixels, so…","u":"glossary.html#pipeline-stage","w":"stage"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pipelining","s":"Working on several frames at once in different stages, like an assembly line: the game thread updates one frame while the render thread…","u":"glossary.html#pipelining","w":"frame pipelining parallel rendering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"PIX on Windows","s":"Microsoft's performance tuning and debugging tool for Direct3D 12 games on Windows and Xbox. GPU captures debug and time one frame; timing…","u":"glossary.html#pix","w":"PIX GPU capture (PIX) Timing capture .wpix"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pixel","s":"One dot of the final image. A 1920 by 1080 frame has about 2.07 million of them.","u":"glossary.html#pixel","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pixel coverage","s":"Whether a triangle covers a pixel, decided by testing the pixel's sample point against the triangle's three edges. A pixel is either fully…","u":"glossary.html#pixel-coverage","w":"coverage coverage test inside test"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pixel quad","s":"A 2 by 2 block of pixels that the GPU always shades together, so the shader can compare neighbours. Pixels in the block that the triangle…","u":"glossary.html#pixel-quad","w":"2×2 quad quad helper lane helper pixel helper invocation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pixel shader","s":"The GPU program that runs for every pixel a triangle covers and decides that pixel's colour.","u":"glossary.html#pixel-shader","w":"fragment shader"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pixels per triangle","s":"The pixels an object covers on screen divided by the triangles drawn for it. As it falls toward one or less, extra triangles add no…","u":"glossary.html#pixels-per-triangle","w":"triangle density triangle size on screen"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Planar reflection","s":"An exact reflection for a flat surface made by rendering the scene again from a camera mirrored below the plane. It sees everything,…","u":"glossary.html#planar-reflection","w":"Planar Reflection Probe mirror reflection planar reflections"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Platform","s":"Everything a game runs on and sells through: the hardware, its operating system, its store and its owner's rules. PS5, Windows PCs,…","u":"glossary.html#platform","w":"target platform target"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Platform holder","s":"The company that controls a closed platform and its store, such as Sony, Microsoft, Nintendo or Meta, and decides which games may be sold…","u":"glossary.html#platform-holder","w":"platform owner first party console manufacturer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Platform layer","s":"The bottom layer of an engine that wraps each operating system's way of opening windows, reading controllers, accessing files, keeping…","u":"glossary.html#platform-layer","w":"platform abstraction layer HAL OS layer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Plugin","s":"A self-contained bundle of code and content that adds a feature to an engine without changing the engine itself. Engines split their own…","u":"glossary.html#plugin","w":"module package addon extension GDExtension"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Point","s":"A place, measured from an origin. Subtracting one point from another gives the vector between them, and adding a vector to a point moves…","u":"glossary.html#point","w":"position location"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Point light","s":"A light that shines equally in all directions from a single point, like a bare bulb. Its light weakens with the square of the distance.","u":"glossary.html#point-light","w":"omni light OmniLight3D Point Light punctual light"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Polygon count","s":"How many triangles a mesh or a whole frame contains. Teams set budgets per asset and per frame for each platform, because every triangle…","u":"glossary.html#polygon-count","w":"poly count polycount triangle count tri count"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Parallax occlusion mapping","s":"Stepping along the view ray through a height map in every pixel until it meets the surface, so raised detail shifts with the view and…","u":"glossary.html#pom","w":"POM Deep Parallax pixel displacement"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Portal culling","s":"Visibility for indoor levels split into rooms (cells) joined by openings (portals). The renderer only looks into other rooms through the…","u":"glossary.html#portal-culling","w":"cells and portals portals rooms and portals Occlusion Portal"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Post-process anti-aliasing","s":"Anti-aliasing that works on the finished image: it finds edges by contrast and blends pixels along them. It is cheap and works with any…","u":"glossary.html#post-process-aa","w":"morphological anti-aliasing MLAA screen-space anti-aliasing Screen Space AA post AA"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Post-process volume","s":"A box in the level, or a global setting, that holds values for post-processing effects. The engine blends the values of overlapping…","u":"glossary.html#post-process-volume","w":"Post Process Volume Volume Volume override Volume Profile WorldEnvironment"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Post-processing","s":"Full-screen passes that run after the scene is lit, reading the lit image plus buffers such as depth and motion vectors and writing a new…","u":"glossary.html#post-processing","w":"post effects post-process post FX full-screen effects"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Power budget","s":"How much electrical power a device can draw and how much heat it can shed. It limits sustained performance far more tightly on phones and…","u":"glossary.html#power-budget","w":"thermal budget TDP"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Power-of-two size","s":"A texture side of 256, 512, 1024, 2048 or another power of two. Such textures halve cleanly into smaller copies and suit compressed…","u":"glossary.html#power-of-two","w":"POT power of 2 NPOT non-power-of-two"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pre-production","s":"The phase after concept where a small team tests the riskiest ideas, proves the look and the pipeline, sets budgets and plans the rest of…","u":"glossary.html#pre-production","w":"preproduction"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Precomputed real-time GI","s":"GI that precomputes how much static surface patches see each other, then solves bounced light from moving or changing lights at run time,…","u":"glossary.html#precomputed-realtime-gi","w":"Enlighten Enlighten Realtime Global Illumination Realtime GI"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Prefab","s":"A saved, reusable object with its components and children that can be placed many times. Editing the prefab updates every placed copy.","u":"glossary.html#prefab","w":"Blueprint class packed scene template"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Prefab instance","s":"One placed copy of a prefab. It stays linked to the prefab, so it picks up changes made to it.","u":"glossary.html#prefab-instance","w":"instance instanced scene"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Prefab variant","s":"A prefab based on another prefab that changes some of its values and inherits the rest, such as a mossy crate made from the plain one.","u":"glossary.html#prefab-variant","w":"inherited scene child Blueprint"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Prefiltered environment map","s":"An environment map whose mip levels hold ever blurrier copies, one per roughness, so a rough surface can read its blurred reflection in a…","u":"glossary.html#prefiltered-environment-map","w":"prefiltered cubemap radiance map specular convolution convolved cubemap"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Premultiplied alpha","s":"Storing colour already multiplied by its alpha, and blending with new + (1 − alpha) × old. It filters without dark or bright halos and…","u":"glossary.html#premultiplied-alpha","w":"premultiply AlphaComposite associated alpha"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Present","s":"The call that hands a finished frame to the display system, moving it to the front of the swap chain at the next allowed moment.","u":"glossary.html#present","w":"Present() vkQueuePresentKHR flip swap"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Primitive assembly","s":"The fixed step after the vertex shader that joins shaded corners into triangles, drops triangles facing away from the camera, and clips…","u":"glossary.html#primitive-assembly","w":"triangle assembly clipping and culling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Producer","s":"The person who plans and tracks the work: schedule, scope, priorities and who does what next. The creative and technical decisions inside…","u":"glossary.html#producer","w":"project manager"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Production","s":"The main phase of development, when the full team builds the game's features and content to the plan and budgets set in pre-production.","u":"glossary.html#production","w":"full production"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Production readiness","s":"Whether an asset or change meets everything needed to ship: technical budgets and formats, art direction, platform rules and the right to…","u":"glossary.html#production-readiness","w":"production-ready ship-ready shippable"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Profiler","s":"A tool that measures where time goes, on the CPU, the GPU or both, frame by frame. Frame capture tools such as RenderDoc and PIX are one…","u":"glossary.html#profiler","w":"profiling tool performance profiler"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Programmable stage","s":"A pipeline stage that runs a shader written for the game, such as the vertex shader or the pixel shader.","u":"glossary.html#programmable-stage","w":"shader stage"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Projection matrix","s":"The matrix that takes view-space positions into clip space. It holds the field of view, aspect ratio and near and far planes, and makes…","u":"glossary.html#projection-matrix","w":"P camera projection"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Prototype","s":"A quick, disposable build made to answer one question, such as whether a mechanic is fun or whether an effect can run on the target…","u":"glossary.html#prototype","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Pipeline state object","s":"A bundle of compiled shaders plus the fixed GPU settings they run with, such as vertex layout, blend, depth and stencil state and target…","u":"glossary.html#pso","w":"PSO pipeline graphics pipeline"},
  {"k":"term","t":"PSO cache","s":"Ways of creating pipeline state objects before they are needed and keeping the results on disk, so later runs and later draws do not stall…","u":"glossary.html#pso-cache","w":"pipeline cache PSO precaching shader warm-up prewarming bundled PSO cache"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Potentially visible set","s":"For each region of a level, a list computed ahead of time of every object that could be seen from somewhere in it. At runtime the camera's…","u":"glossary.html#pvs","w":"PVS precomputed visibility visibility cells"},
  {"k":"term","t":"QA","s":"Quality assurance: the people who test every build, find and report bugs, check fixes and confirm the game works on each target platform.","u":"glossary.html#qa","w":"quality assurance tester QA tester"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Quad","s":"A four-sided face. Artists model mostly in quads because they form clean rows of edges that are easy to select, edit and subdivide; each…","u":"glossary.html#quad","w":"quadrilateral quad modelling quad topology"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Quad overdraw","s":"Extra pixel shader runs caused by 2 by 2 quads that triangles only partly cover, especially along shared edges and on small or thin…","u":"glossary.html#quad-overdraw","w":"quad overshading Quad Overdraw view"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Upscaler quality mode","s":"A named ratio between display and internal resolution per axis, such as Quality at 1.5 times or Performance at 2 times. Vendors use the…","u":"glossary.html#quality-mode","w":"quality mode quality preset Quality Balanced Performance Ultra Performance DLAA Native AA"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Quality preset","s":"A named bundle of graphics settings, such as Low, Medium, High or Ultra, that sets every option at once. Games choose a default preset per…","u":"glossary.html#quality-preset","w":"graphics preset scalability tier overall quality Low, Medium, High, Ultra quality level"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex quantization","s":"Storing vertex attributes with fewer bits, for example 16-bit instead of 32-bit numbers, to save memory and bandwidth at a small loss of…","u":"glossary.html#quantization","w":"vertex compression mesh compression packing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RACI","s":"A grid of decisions or tasks against people that marks who is Responsible for doing the work, Accountable for the result, Consulted before…","u":"glossary.html#raci","w":"RACI matrix RACI chart responsibility assignment matrix"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Radeon GPU Profiler","s":"AMD's low-level GPU profiler for DirectX 12 and Vulkan on Radeon GPUs. It shows when each event ran, how full the GPU was, and which…","u":"glossary.html#radeon-gpu-profiler","w":"RGP Radeon Developer Tool Suite Radeon Developer Panel"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Radiance","s":"The light travelling along one direction, which is what a camera pixel or an eye measures. Luminance, measured in nits, is its everyday…","u":"glossary.html#radiance","w":"L outgoing radiance incoming radiance"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Radiance cascades","s":"A GI method by Alexander Sannikov that stores light in a stack of probe grids: each level has probes twice as far apart, covers rays twice…","u":"glossary.html#radiance-cascades","w":"radiance cascade"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Radiosity","s":"A way to compute bounced light between matte surfaces: cut them into patches, work out how much of each patch's light reaches every other,…","u":"glossary.html#radiosity","w":"radiosity method"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Rasterization","s":"The fixed GPU step that finds which pixels a triangle covers and gives each of them a blend of the values at the triangle's three corners.","u":"glossary.html#rasterization","w":"rasterisation scan conversion"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Rasterizer","s":"The fixed GPU stage that finds which pixels each triangle covers and hands each one to the pixel shader with values blended from the…","u":"glossary.html#rasterizer","w":"rasterizer stage raster stage"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray","s":"A starting point, a direction and a range of distances to search: the question of what is the first thing in that direction. Ray tracing…","u":"glossary.html#ray","w":"rays ray origin ray direction tMin tMax"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray budget","s":"How many rays an effect may trace per pixel per frame. Real-time effects typically use a quarter of a ray to one ray per pixel and rely on…","u":"glossary.html#ray-budget","w":"rays per pixel rpp ray count"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Coherence","s":"How alike neighbouring rays are. Rays with similar origins and directions visit the same boxes and hit the same materials, so GPU lanes…","u":"glossary.html#ray-coherence","w":"coherent rays ray coherence"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Divergence","s":"Neighbouring rays going different ways: they visit different parts of the tree and hit different materials, so lanes in one warp wait for…","u":"glossary.html#ray-divergence","w":"divergent rays incoherent rays execution divergence"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray marching","s":"Following a ray in small steps and checking at each step whether it has gone behind a surface, often using the depth buffer.","u":"glossary.html#ray-marching","w":"screen-space tracing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray-miss fallback","s":"What a screen-space effect uses for a ray that leaves the screen or finds nothing: a reflection or light probe, baked lighting, the sky,…","u":"glossary.html#ray-miss-fallback","w":"Ray Miss fallback fallback chain"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray reconstruction","s":"NVIDIA's neural network, introduced with DLSS 3.5 in 2023, that replaces a game's hand-tuned denoisers for ray-traced lighting with one…","u":"glossary.html#ray-reconstruction","w":"DLSS Ray Reconstruction DLSS-RR DLSS 3.5"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray-traced reflections","s":"Reflections found by tracing the mirror direction, spread by roughness, against the real scene, so objects that are off screen or hidden…","u":"glossary.html#ray-traced-reflections","w":"RT reflections RTR"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray-traced shadows","s":"Shadows found by tracing a ray from each pixel's surface towards the light and stopping at the first hit. Aiming at random points on an…","u":"glossary.html#ray-traced-shadows","w":"RT shadows Cast Ray Traced Shadows"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray tracing","s":"Finding what a ray hits by testing it against the actual scene geometry, using acceleration structures that GPUs can now traverse in…","u":"glossary.html#ray-tracing","w":"RT hardware ray tracing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ray-triangle intersection","s":"The test of whether a ray passes through a triangle: find where it crosses the triangle's plane, then check that the point's three…","u":"glossary.html#ray-triangle-intersection","w":"ray-triangle test Möller–Trumbore triangle test"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Rayleigh scattering","s":"Scattering by particles much smaller than the wavelength of light, such as air molecules. It is far stronger for short wavelengths, which…","u":"glossary.html#rayleigh-scattering","w":"Rayleigh"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Real-time rendering","s":"Drawing images fast enough to keep up with the player, typically 30 to well over 100 images a second. Offline rendering for film can…","u":"glossary.html#real-time-rendering","w":"real-time graphics interactive rendering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Recommended specification","s":"The hardware a PC game suggests for the experience its makers intend, often given with a resolution, frame rate and settings preset.","u":"glossary.html#recommended-spec","w":"recommended requirements rec spec"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Reference rendering","s":"An image rendered with enough samples to be correct, used to judge real-time approximations and tune them. Engine path tracers exist…","u":"glossary.html#reference-rendering","w":"reference image ground truth converged image"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Reflection fallback","s":"The order in which reflection methods answer a pixel when the more accurate one has nothing, for example screen space, then a reflection…","u":"glossary.html#reflection-fallback","w":"reflection hierarchy fallback chain reflection fallback order"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Reflection probe","s":"An environment map captured at a chosen point in a level and applied to surfaces within its area, so each area reflects its own…","u":"glossary.html#reflection-probe","w":"reflection capture ReflectionProbe Sphere Reflection Capture Box Reflection Capture local light probe cubemap probe"},
  {"k":"term","t":"NVIDIA Reflex","s":"NVIDIA's latency reducer: it paces the CPU so frames do not queue up waiting for the GPU, cutting the time from input to screen. DLSS…","u":"glossary.html#reflex","w":"Reflex Reflex Low Latency Reflex Low Latency mode"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Refraction","s":"Light bending as it passes from one material into another, as at a water or glass surface. Games fake it by reading a copy of the already…","u":"glossary.html#refraction","w":"screen-space refraction distortion refracted colour"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Refresh rate","s":"How many times per second a display redraws its picture, in hertz (Hz). A 60 Hz display starts a new picture every 16.7 ms; a 144 Hz…","u":"glossary.html#refresh-rate","w":"Hz hertz refresh"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Register pressure","s":"A shader needing many registers to hold its live values. Registers are shared by all the warps on a compute unit, so heavier shaders leave…","u":"glossary.html#register-pressure","w":"VGPR pressure register usage registers per thread"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Render pass","s":"One step in drawing a frame that reads some images and writes others, such as the shadow pass, the AO pass or the lighting pass.","u":"glossary.html#render-pass","w":"pass"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Render pipeline","s":"The part of an engine that decides how each frame is drawn: which passes run, in what order and with which lighting. In Unity it is a…","u":"glossary.html#render-pipeline","w":"Scriptable Render Pipeline SRP rendering pipeline"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Render state","s":"The settings the GPU needs in place before a draw: which shaders run, which buffers and textures are bound, and how blending, depth…","u":"glossary.html#render-state","w":"pipeline state state change GPU state SetPass call"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Render target","s":"An image the GPU draws into instead of, or before, the screen, such as a shadow map, a G-buffer layer or an HDR colour buffer. Later…","u":"glossary.html#render-target","w":"RT colour attachment render texture framebuffer attachment"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Render target format","s":"The pixel format of an image the GPU draws into, such as RGBA8 or R11G11B10F at 4 bytes per pixel or RGBA16F at 8. It sets the memory and…","u":"glossary.html#render-target-format","w":"pixel format colour buffer format Color Buffer Format HDR Precision"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Render thread","s":"A CPU thread that takes a copy of what the game thread produced and turns it into work for the GPU, while the game thread is already…","u":"glossary.html#render-thread","w":"rendering thread RenderThread"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RenderDoc","s":"A free, open-source frame debugger for Vulkan, Direct3D 11, Direct3D 12, OpenGL and OpenGL ES on Windows, Linux and Android. It records a…","u":"glossary.html#renderdoc","w":"RenderDoc capture .rdc"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Renderer","s":"The part of an engine that turns the current state of the game world into an image each frame. It decides what is visible, organises the…","u":"glossary.html#renderer","w":"rendering engine render pipeline rendering server"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Rendering equation","s":"James Kajiya's 1986 equation for light leaving a point: what it emits, plus the light arriving from every direction times the material's…","u":"glossary.html#rendering-equation","w":"light transport equation Kajiya's equation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Rendering method","s":"Godot's choice of renderer for a project: Forward+ for desktop, Mobile for phones and standalone VR, or Compatibility for older hardware…","u":"glossary.html#rendering-method","w":"Godot renderer Mobile renderer Compatibility renderer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Rendering path","s":"How a renderer organises the work of lighting a frame: forward, deferred, or either with tiled or clustered light lists. It sets how many…","u":"glossary.html#rendering-path","w":"render path rendering mode lighting path"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Reproduction steps","s":"The exact build, settings, place, camera and actions that make a problem appear every time, so someone else can see it and later confirm…","u":"glossary.html#reproduction-steps","w":"repro steps repro steps to reproduce minimal reproduction project MRP"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Reprojection","s":"Working out where each pixel's surface was on screen last frame, from motion vectors or from depth and the camera's movement, and reading…","u":"glossary.html#reprojection","w":"history reprojection reproject"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Resolution","s":"The number of pixels in an image, written width × height. 1920 × 1080 (1080p) is about 2.07 million pixels; 3840 × 2160 (4K) is four times…","u":"glossary.html#resolution","w":"output resolution display resolution 1080p 1440p 4K"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Resolution scaling","s":"How a pass's cost changes with the number of pixels rendered. Per-pixel work costs four times as much at 4K as at 1080p, while shadow…","u":"glossary.html#resolution-scaling","w":"resolution test resolution-sensitive per-pixel cost"},
  {"k":"term","t":"ReSTIR","s":"A 2020 technique that picks a good light sample for each pixel by resampling many cheap candidates, then shares the winners with…","u":"glossary.html#restir","w":"reservoir-based spatiotemporal importance resampling ReSTIR DI ReSTIR GI ReSTIR PT RTXDI"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Retopology","s":"Building a new, lighter mesh over a detailed sculpt, with edges placed where the shape and animation need them, so the model is cheap…","u":"glossary.html#retopology","w":"retopo remeshing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Reversed-Z","s":"Storing depth with the near plane at 1 and the far distance at 0, in a 32-bit floating-point depth buffer. It makes depth precision almost…","u":"glossary.html#reversed-z","w":"reverse Z reversed depth inverted depth buffer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RGB","s":"Describing a colour as three amounts of light: red, green and blue. Every pixel on a screen mixes those three lights to make the colours…","u":"glossary.html#rgb","w":"red, green, blue RGB colour"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RHI","s":"The layer that gives the renderer one set of graphics commands and translates them into whichever graphics API the platform uses, such as…","u":"glossary.html#rhi","w":"render hardware interface Rendering Hardware Interface RenderingDevice graphics abstraction layer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RHI thread","s":"In Unreal Engine, a third CPU thread that translates the render thread's platform-neutral command lists into DirectX 12, Vulkan or Metal…","u":"glossary.html#rhi-thread","w":"RHIT RHIThread"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Roughness","s":"A material input from 0 (mirror-smooth) to 1 (fully matte) that sets how much a surface's microfacets disagree, and so how wide and dim…","u":"glossary.html#roughness","w":"smoothness glossiness gloss"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Royalty","s":"A percentage of a game's revenue paid to the engine maker. Unreal Engine charges one on a game's gross revenue above its first $1 million.","u":"glossary.html#royalty","w":"revenue share"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RT cores","s":"GPU hardware that tests rays against boxes and triangles so shader cores do not have to. NVIDIA's RT cores also walk the BVH; AMD's ray…","u":"glossary.html#rt-cores","w":"ray accelerators ray tracing units RT units ray tracing hardware"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RTAO","s":"Ambient occlusion computed by tracing short rays against the real scene geometry instead of reading the depth buffer, so it sees hidden…","u":"glossary.html#rtao","w":"ray-traced ambient occlusion"},
  {"k":"term","t":"RTGI","s":"Bounced light gathered by tracing rays from surfaces or probes into the scene, usually with few rays per pixel and the results shared…","u":"glossary.html#rtgi","w":"ray-traced global illumination ray-traced GI"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Runtime","s":"The part of an engine that ships inside the game and runs on players' machines. It contains the systems that run each frame, without the…","u":"glossary.html#runtime","w":"player game build shipping build"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Sample point","s":"The single point at which the GPU tests whether a triangle covers a pixel. Without multisampling it is the pixel's centre, half a pixel in…","u":"glossary.html#sample-point","w":"pixel centre pixel center sample location"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Samples per pixel","s":"How many random paths or rays are averaged for each pixel. Noise falls with its square root: 4 times the samples give half the noise.","u":"glossary.html#samples-per-pixel","w":"spp SPP paths per pixel Samples Per Pixel Maximum Samples"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Scalability","s":"The settings that let one game trade visual quality for speed and memory, grouped into presets for each platform and device tier.","u":"glossary.html#scalability","w":"quality settings quality levels presets scalability settings"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Scalability group","s":"One family of settings in Unreal's scalability system, such as shadows, global illumination, reflections or post-processing. Each has…","u":"glossary.html#scalability-group","w":"sg.ShadowQuality sg.GlobalIlluminationQuality quality group Scalability Groups"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Scan-out","s":"The display reading its picture out of the front buffer row by row, from the top of the screen to the bottom, during each refresh.","u":"glossary.html#scan-out","w":"scanout raster scan"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Scene","s":"One saved world or piece of a world, such as a level, a menu or a test room, holding every object placed in it. Unreal Engine calls it a…","u":"glossary.html#scene","w":"level map scene file .umap .unity .tscn"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Scene graph","s":"The tree of every object in a scene, where each object can have children that move with it. Editors show it as the Hierarchy, the Outliner…","u":"glossary.html#scene-graph","w":"scene hierarchy hierarchy scene tree Outliner"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Scene-referred","s":"Describing values as amounts of light in the scene, with no upper limit. Lighting, exposure and high-precision grading work on…","u":"glossary.html#scene-referred","w":"scene referred scene-linear values"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Screen edge fade","s":"Blending screen-space hits away as they approach the edge of the frame, so a reflection or bounce fades out instead of popping when its…","u":"glossary.html#screen-edge-fade","w":"Screen Edge Fade Distance edge fade border fade"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Screen probes","s":"Lumen's final gather: probes placed on the visible surfaces, about one per 16 × 16 pixels, each gathering light from 64 directions. Their…","u":"glossary.html#screen-probes","w":"Screen Probe Gather screen space radiance cache final gather"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Screen size","s":"How large an object appears on screen, usually its height as a fraction of the screen's height. Engines switch levels of detail when it…","u":"glossary.html#screen-size","w":"screen coverage % screen size screen relative transition height LOD threshold"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Screen space","s":"Working from the rendered image and its depth rather than the 3D scene. Screen-space techniques are fast, but they only know about what…","u":"glossary.html#screen-space","w":"image space"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Screen-space derivatives","s":"How fast a value changes from one pixel to the next, measured by comparing neighbours in a 2 by 2 quad. Texture sampling uses them to pick…","u":"glossary.html#screen-space-derivatives","w":"derivatives ddx ddy dFdx dFdy fwidth"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Screen tearing","s":"A horizontal break across the picture where the rows above show one frame and the rows below show the next, because the frame was swapped…","u":"glossary.html#screen-tearing","w":"tearing tear line"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Scripting layer","s":"The language and interface gameplay programmers and designers use to write a game's logic on top of the engine, such as Blueprints, C# in…","u":"glossary.html#scripting-layer","w":"scripting gameplay scripting Blueprints GDScript C# scripts"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SDFGI","s":"Godot's semi-real-time GI. It builds cascades of signed distance fields of static geometry around the camera, each cascade with cells…","u":"glossary.html#sdfgi","w":"signed distance field global illumination"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Seamless streaming","s":"Loading the world around the player in the background while they play, so there are no loading screens. It needs less memory at any…","u":"glossary.html#seamless-streaming","w":"open-world streaming no loading screens background streaming"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Seat licence","s":"A licence paid for each person who uses the editor, usually yearly, whatever the game earns. Unity charges this way once a company passes…","u":"glossary.html#seat-licence","w":"per-seat subscription seat"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Self time","s":"The time a function spends in its own code, leaving out the functions it calls. Inclusive time adds them back, so ranking by self time…","u":"glossary.html#self-time","w":"exclusive time Self self ms"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shader","s":"A small program that runs on the GPU for every vertex, pixel or work item, in parallel.","u":"glossary.html#shader","w":"shader program"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shader compilation","s":"Turning shader source into GPU machine code in two stages: into an in-between form such as DXIL or SPIR-V when the game is built, then…","u":"glossary.html#shader-compilation","w":"shader compile shader compiling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shader complexity view","s":"An engine debug view that colours each pixel by how much shading it costs, from cheap to expensive, so overlapping or heavy materials…","u":"glossary.html#shader-complexity-view","w":"Shader Complexity overdraw view Display Overdraw"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shader graph","s":"A node-based editor where artists build shaders by wiring boxes together. The engine turns the graph into ordinary shader code and…","u":"glossary.html#shader-graph","w":"Material Editor Shader Graph Visual Shader node-based shader editor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shader compilation stutter","s":"A hitch the first time something is drawn, because its pipeline state object is created and its shaders compiled by the driver at that…","u":"glossary.html#shader-stutter","w":"PSO stutter shader hitch compile hitch first-use hitch"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shader variant","s":"One compiled version of a shader for one combination of on/off features. Each independent toggle doubles the count, so five toggles make…","u":"glossary.html#shader-variant","w":"shader permutation keyword variant static switch"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shading model","s":"The formula a renderer uses to turn the light arriving at a point, the surface's normal and material, and the direction of the camera into…","u":"glossary.html#shading-model","w":"lighting model"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow acne","s":"Stripes or speckles of false shadow on lit surfaces, caused by a surface failing the shadow test against its own stored depth, because…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-acne","w":"self-shadowing erroneous self-shadowing shadow moiré"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow atlas","s":"One large texture holding the shadow maps of many lights in separate regions, usually with bigger regions for lights that are larger on…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-atlas","w":"shadow map atlas Shadow Atlas Resolution positional shadow atlas"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow bias","s":"A small offset that moves the shadow test towards the light so surfaces do not shadow themselves. Too little leaves shadow acne; too much…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-bias","w":"depth bias constant bias depth offset"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow caching","s":"Keeping a shadow map from earlier frames and rendering it again only when the light or something casting into it changes. It saves the…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-caching","w":"cached shadows shadow update mode On Demand shadows"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow caster","s":"An object drawn into shadow maps, so it can block light. Whether an object casts shadows is a separate setting from whether it receives…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-caster","w":"caster cast shadows shadow-casting object"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow distance","s":"How far from the camera real-time shadows reach. It sets the far end of the last cascade, so shortening it makes every cascade's texels…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-distance","w":"Max Distance Dynamic Shadow Distance shadow draw distance"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow map","s":"A depth image rendered from a light's point of view, storing for each texel the distance to the first surface the light meets. Any point…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-map","w":"shadow depth map shadowmap"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow pass","s":"The render pass that draws every shadow caster into a light's shadow map, once per map or cube face. Its cost grows with the casters…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-pass","w":"shadow depth pass shadow map pass shadow rendering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow map resolution","s":"How many texels a shadow map has, and so how much of the world each texel covers. No shadow edge can be sharper than one texel.","u":"glossary.html#shadow-resolution","w":"shadow resolution shadow texel density shadow map size"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow test","s":"Projecting a pixel's position into the shadow map and comparing its light-space depth with the stored depth: if the pixel is further from…","u":"glossary.html#shadow-test","w":"shadow comparison shadow lookup"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Shadow mask","s":"A baked texture that stores how much each texel is shadowed from a particular light, so the light's direct lighting can be computed every…","u":"glossary.html#shadowmask","w":"Shadowmask Distance Shadowmask distance field shadow map shadowmask_mode"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Sharpening","s":"A filter that steepens edges by pushing each side away from the other, used after TAA and upscalers to restore crispness. Too much draws…","u":"glossary.html#sharpening","w":"sharpen filter Contrast Adaptive Sharpening CAS RCAS Post Sharpen unsharp mask"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Signed distance field","s":"A volume storing, at each point, the distance to the nearest surface: positive outside, negative inside. A ray can safely jump that far at…","u":"glossary.html#signed-distance-field","w":"SDF distance field mesh distance field Global Distance Field"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Significance","s":"A score for how much an object matters to the player right now, from its distance, visibility or role. Games use it to update less…","u":"glossary.html#significance","w":"Significance Manager logic LOD update LOD tick LOD update rate optimisation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SIMD and SIMT","s":"Running many lanes from one stream of instructions: the hardware issues each instruction once and every lane in a group applies it to its…","u":"glossary.html#simd","w":"SIMD SIMT single instruction, multiple data single instruction, multiple threads"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Skeleton","s":"A hierarchy of bones that animation poses every frame and that a skinned mesh follows. Each bone is placed relative to its parent, so…","u":"glossary.html#skeleton","w":"rig armature Skeleton3D Skeleton asset"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Skin weights","s":"Numbers stored per vertex saying how strongly each of a few bones moves it. They add up to 1 and are painted by riggers.","u":"glossary.html#skin-weights","w":"bone weights vertex weights weight painting influences"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Skinned mesh","s":"A mesh whose vertices also store bone numbers and skin weights, so that it can follow a skeleton.","u":"glossary.html#skinned-mesh","w":"Skeletal Mesh Skinned Mesh Renderer skinned model"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Skinning","s":"Moving every vertex of a mesh each frame to follow the bones of its skeleton, according to each vertex's skin weights.","u":"glossary.html#skinning","w":"skeletal deformation vertex skinning mesh deformation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Sky atmosphere model","s":"A renderer's simulation of light scattering through a planet's atmosphere from its Rayleigh, Mie and absorption settings. It gives the…","u":"glossary.html#sky-atmosphere","w":"physically based sky Sky Atmosphere Physically Based Sky PhysicalSkyMaterial atmospheric scattering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Sky light","s":"Light arriving from the whole surrounding environment rather than one point, such as the blue sky on a sunny day or a uniform grey overcast.","u":"glossary.html#sky-light","w":"ambient light environment light"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Slice","s":"In GTAO, one direction across the screen through a pixel. The pixel's hemisphere is examined one thin vertical slice at a time.","u":"glossary.html#slice","w":"slice direction"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Slope-scaled bias","s":"Shadow bias that grows with how steeply a surface is tilted against the light, where one texel spans the largest change in depth. It gives…","u":"glossary.html#slope-scaled-bias","w":"slope bias slope-scale depth bias SlopeScaledDepthBias"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SMAA","s":"Post-process anti-aliasing by Jorge Jimenez and colleagues (2012) that reads the shape of stair-step patterns in three passes and blends…","u":"glossary.html#smaa","w":"Subpixel Morphological Anti-Aliasing Enhanced Subpixel Morphological Antialiasing SMAA 1x SMAA T2x"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Small-object culling","s":"Skipping objects whose projected size on screen falls below a threshold, such as a few pixels, because they would barely change the image.","u":"glossary.html#small-object-culling","w":"screen-size culling contribution culling screen size culling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Small-triangle cost","s":"The extra GPU work caused by triangles only a few pixels in size: each still needs setup and shades whole 2 by 2 pixel quads, so up to…","u":"glossary.html#small-triangle-cost","w":"micro-triangles micropolygons tiny triangles"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Soft particles","s":"Fading a particle where it comes close to the surface behind it, using the difference between its depth and the depth buffer, so flat…","u":"glossary.html#soft-particles","w":"depth fade DepthFade Proximity Fade Soft Particles"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Software ray tracing","s":"Lumen's tracing mode that marches rays through signed distance fields of the scene instead of its triangles. It runs on any Shader Model 6…","u":"glossary.html#software-ray-tracing","w":"Lumen Software Ray Tracing SWRT distance field tracing Global Tracing Detail Tracing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Source asset","s":"The editable original of a piece of content, such as a sculpt or a layered texture project. The team keeps and versions it; players never…","u":"glossary.html#source-asset","w":"source file source art working file"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Source-available","s":"Source code you may read and change under the vendor's licence, but not share or reuse freely. Unreal Engine's full source is available…","u":"glossary.html#source-available","w":"source access engine source"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Sparse texture","s":"A texture with only some of its memory pages backed by real memory, using the GPU's own memory mapping. It is the hardware support behind…","u":"glossary.html#sparse-texture","w":"tiled resources sparse residency reserved resources partially resident texture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Spatial upscaling","s":"Enlarging one rendered frame to the display resolution by interpolating between its pixels, sometimes with edge-aware sharpening. Cheap,…","u":"glossary.html#spatial-upscaling","w":"spatial upscaler FSR 1 bilinear upscale Catmull-Rom upscale MetalFX Spatial"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Specular reflection","s":"Light reflected in a preferred direction, like a mirror or a highlight on polished metal. It changes as the viewer moves.","u":"glossary.html#specular","w":"specular highlights"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Specular aliasing","s":"Flickering bright pixels on shiny, bumpy or finely curved surfaces, where highlights smaller than a pixel are caught by some pixels'…","u":"glossary.html#specular-aliasing","w":"specular shimmer shading aliasing sparkling highlights"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Specular GI","s":"Bounced light seen in reflections, such as a red wall mirrored in a shiny floor. It changes with every camera move and is handled by…","u":"glossary.html#specular-gi","w":"indirect specular specular global illumination reflections"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Specular/glossiness workflow","s":"An older way to author PBR materials, with diffuse, specular colour and glossiness maps. It gives direct control over F0 but makes…","u":"glossary.html#specular-glossiness","w":"spec/gloss specular workflow Specular Color"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Specular highlight","s":"The bright spot where a surface reflects a light source towards the camera, in effect a blurred image of the light. Its size shows how…","u":"glossary.html#specular-highlight","w":"specular blob hotspot"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Specular occlusion","s":"Darkening reflections in creases and cavities where the reflected direction is blocked by nearby geometry.","u":"glossary.html#specular-occlusion","w":"GTSO ground-truth specular occlusion"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Spherical harmonics","s":"A way to describe a quantity that varies smoothly with direction, such as the light arriving at a probe, as a short list of numbers. Nine…","u":"glossary.html#spherical-harmonics","w":"SH L2 SH SH coefficients SH bands third order SH"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SPIR-V","s":"A standard intermediate format for compiled shaders, defined by the Khronos Group. Vulkan drivers read it, and tools translate between it…","u":"glossary.html#spir-v","w":"SPIRV"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Splat map","s":"A texture whose channels hold the weight of each terrain material layer at each point, so grass, rock, sand and snow can be painted and…","u":"glossary.html#splat-map","w":"splatmap weightmap weight map control texture Landscape Layer Blend"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Split-sum approximation","s":"Brian Karis's way of making image-based specular lighting cheap: a prefiltered environment map multiplied by a small precomputed table…","u":"glossary.html#split-sum","w":"split sum environment BRDF DFG LUT BRDF integration map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Split vertex","s":"A corner stored as two or more vertices at the same position because the triangles meeting there need different normals, UVs or colours.","u":"glossary.html#split-vertex","w":"seam hard edge duplicated vertex"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Spot light","s":"A point light limited to a cone, like a torch. It is at full strength inside the inner cone angle and fades to nothing at the outer cone…","u":"glossary.html#spot-light","w":"spotlight Spot Light SpotLight3D cone light"},
  {"k":"term","t":"sRGB","s":"The standard colour space for PC screens and the web. It fixes which red, green and blue the screen uses, and a curve that stores values…","u":"glossary.html#srgb","w":"sRGB transfer function sRGB gamut Rec. 709 primaries IEC 61966-2-1"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SRP Batcher","s":"Unity's draw call optimisation for URP and HDRP. It keeps material data in GPU memory, so consecutive draws using the same shader variant…","u":"glossary.html#srp-batcher","w":"Scriptable Render Pipeline Batcher"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SSAA","s":"Supersample anti-aliasing: rendering and shading the frame at a higher resolution, then averaging it down. It removes every kind of…","u":"glossary.html#ssaa","w":"supersampling supersample anti-aliasing downsampling resolution scale above 100%"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SSAO","s":"Ambient occlusion estimated from the depth buffer by testing a handful of nearby sample points per pixel. Fast, noisy, and limited to what…","u":"glossary.html#ssao","w":"screen-space ambient occlusion"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SSGI","s":"Bounced light estimated by tracing rays through the depth buffer and picking up colour from the lit image. It inherits every screen-space…","u":"glossary.html#ssgi","w":"screen-space global illumination SSIL screen-space indirect lighting"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SSR","s":"Reflections found by tracing rays through the depth buffer and reusing the colour of whatever on-screen pixel they hit.","u":"glossary.html#ssr","w":"screen-space reflections"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Static batching","s":"Combining meshes that never move and share a material into large world-space buffers ahead of time, so they draw with fewer draw calls. It…","u":"glossary.html#static-batching","w":"Batching Static"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Static buffer","s":"A GPU buffer filled once, usually at load time, and then only read. Most level geometry and props use static buffers.","u":"glossary.html#static-buffer","w":"static vertex buffer immutable buffer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Static light","s":"A light whose effect is computed before the game runs and stored, usually in lightmaps. It costs almost nothing at runtime but cannot move…","u":"glossary.html#static-light","w":"baked light Static Baked"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Stencil buffer","s":"A screen-sized buffer holding a small whole number per pixel, usually 8 bits, stored alongside depth. Draws write marks into it and later…","u":"glossary.html#stencil-buffer","w":"stencil custom stencil"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Stencil test","s":"The per-pixel comparison between a draw's reference value and the number stored in the stencil buffer, with rules for how the stored…","u":"glossary.html#stencil-test","w":"stencil compare"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Store action","s":"What a render pass does with an image when it ends: store it to memory for later passes, or discard it (Don't care). Discarding depth and…","u":"glossary.html#store-action","w":"store op storeOp VK_ATTACHMENT_STORE_OP MTLStoreAction Store, Don't care"},
  {"k":"term","t":"STP","s":"Unity 6's built-in software temporal upscaler for URP and HDRP. It needs compute shaders, does not run on OpenGL ES, and turns TAA on.","u":"glossary.html#stp","w":"Spatial-Temporal Post-processing Spatial-Temporal Post-Processing (STP) 1.0"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture streaming pool","s":"The fixed amount of GPU memory a texture streamer may fill. When the mips the camera wants do not fit, it drops the sharpest levels, so…","u":"glossary.html#streaming-pool","w":"texture pool streaming pool pool size r.Streaming.PoolSize"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Streaming starvation","s":"A streaming system that cannot get the memory or read speed it needs, so textures stay blurry, meshes stay at low detail and objects…","u":"glossary.html#streaming-starvation","w":"texture starvation pop-in late streaming blurry textures"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Stride","s":"The number of bytes from the start of one vertex to the start of the next in a buffer. It is the size of one row.","u":"glossary.html#stride","w":"vertex size"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Struct of arrays","s":"Storing each field of many objects in its own packed array, such as all positions together, instead of one record per object. Loops that…","u":"glossary.html#struct-of-arrays","w":"structure of arrays data-oriented layout"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Subpass","s":"A step inside one Vulkan render pass that can read the previous step's output at the same pixel as an input attachment. When the driver…","u":"glossary.html#subpass","w":"Vulkan subpass merged subpasses input attachment multipass native render pass"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Subpixel jitter","s":"A tiny offset, less than a pixel, added to the camera's projection each frame so that every frame samples slightly different points.…","u":"glossary.html#subpixel-jitter","w":"jitter camera jitter projection jitter jitter offset Jitter Scale"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Subsurface scattering","s":"Light entering a surface, wandering inside and leaving at a different point, as in skin, wax, marble and leaves. Games usually blur each…","u":"glossary.html#subsurface-scattering","w":"SSS screen-space subsurface scattering Subsurface Profile Diffusion Profile separable SSS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Surface cache","s":"Lumen's store of material and lighting for the surfaces around the camera, captured onto small rectangles called cards placed around each…","u":"glossary.html#surface-cache","w":"Lumen surface cache Lumen cards cards Lumen Scene"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Sustained performance","s":"The level of work a device can keep up for a whole play session without overheating, below what it reaches in the first few minutes.…","u":"glossary.html#sustained-performance","w":"sustainable performance sustained frame rate steady-state performance"},
  {"k":"term","t":"SVGF","s":"A 2017 denoiser for one-sample-per-pixel path tracing: it accumulates each pixel's history, estimates how noisy each pixel is, and blurs…","u":"glossary.html#svgf","w":"spatiotemporal variance-guided filtering variance-guided filtering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Swap chain","s":"The set of framebuffers, usually two or three, that the graphics API passes in turn between the GPU and the display, together with the…","u":"glossary.html#swap-chain","w":"swapchain flip chain"},
  {"k":"term","t":"TAA","s":"Anti-aliasing that jitters the camera slightly every frame and blends each new frame with the reprojected previous ones.","u":"glossary.html#taa","w":"temporal anti-aliasing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"TAA blur","s":"The softness temporal anti-aliasing adds: history is resampled every frame and filtered over more than a pixel, so fine detail spreads,…","u":"glossary.html#taa-blur","w":"temporal blur TAA softness reprojection blur"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tangent","s":"A direction lying along a surface, aligned with its texture's horizontal axis. Normal maps need it to know which way is which.","u":"glossary.html#tangent","w":"tangent vector tangent frame"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tangent space","s":"A frame of reference sitting on the surface at each point, made of the normal, the tangent and the bitangent. Normal maps are usually…","u":"glossary.html#tangent-space","w":"TBN tangent basis texture space"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Technical artist","s":"A specialist who works between art and programming. Technical artists build materials, shaders, tools and import rules, and make sure art…","u":"glossary.html#technical-artist","w":"TA tech artist"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Temporal accumulation","s":"Spreading work over several frames: each frame computes a few new samples and blends them with the result from previous frames, moved to…","u":"glossary.html#temporal-accumulation","w":"history temporal filter temporal reprojection"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Temporal upscaling","s":"Rendering fewer pixels with a jittered camera and accumulating the samples of several frames into a full-resolution history, so the output…","u":"glossary.html#temporal-upscaling","w":"temporal upscaler super resolution TAAU TAA Upscale MetalFX Temporal"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Terrain","s":"Ground built from a height map: a grid of vertices raised by the map's values, split into sections so distant parts use fewer triangles,…","u":"glossary.html#terrain","w":"heightmap terrain Landscape Terrain (Unity) heightfield"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tessellation","s":"Splitting triangles into many smaller ones on the GPU at draw time, often to add displaced detail up close. Direct3D 11 added hardware…","u":"glossary.html#tessellation","w":"hardware tessellation hull and domain shaders tessellation shaders"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texel","s":"One value in a texture's grid, the texture's equivalent of a pixel. Texels rarely line up one to one with screen pixels, so the GPU…","u":"glossary.html#texel","w":"texture element texture pixel"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texel density","s":"How many texels cover each metre of a surface: the texels across a UV island divided by the metres across its face. Assets seen together…","u":"glossary.html#texel-density","w":"texels per metre px/m pixels per metre"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture","s":"An image the GPU reads while shading, such as a colour, normal or roughness map. Also used to store data like depth or AO.","u":"glossary.html#texture","w":"map"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture aliasing","s":"Noise, false patterns and flicker that appear when one pixel covers many texels but reads only a few of them. Mipmaps are the standard cure.","u":"glossary.html#texture-aliasing","w":"shimmer moiré sparkle crawling"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture array","s":"A stack of textures of the same size and format, read with UV coordinates plus a layer number that the shader can choose for each pixel.","u":"glossary.html#texture-array","w":"2D array texture Texture2DArray array texture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture atlas","s":"One texture holding the images of several objects in separate regions, so they can share one texture and one material. Regions cannot tile…","u":"glossary.html#texture-atlas","w":"atlas sprite sheet"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture-bound","s":"A GPU pass limited by the texture units that fetch and filter texels, as in blurs and screen-space effects that take many samples. Fewer…","u":"glossary.html#texture-bound","w":"texture-fetch-bound sampler-bound TEX-bound"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture memory budget","s":"The share of the weakest target's memory that a game's textures may use, after render targets, geometry and everything else. Texture…","u":"glossary.html#texture-budget","w":"texture budget Memory Budget VRAM budget"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture filtering","s":"How the GPU turns the texels around a sample point into one value when texels and pixels are different sizes. The usual modes are nearest,…","u":"glossary.html#texture-filtering","w":"filtering filter mode sampler filter"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture resolution","s":"A texture's width and height in texels, such as 2048 × 2048. Memory grows with the square of the side, so doubling it costs four times as…","u":"glossary.html#texture-resolution","w":"texture size texture dimensions max size"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Texture streaming","s":"Loading and unloading parts of textures while the game runs, so memory holds only the detail the camera currently needs and the rest stays…","u":"glossary.html#texture-streaming","w":"texture streamer streaming"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Thermal headroom","s":"How close a device is to throttling, as reported to the game: Android gives a forecast from 0 to 1, where 1 means severe throttling, and…","u":"glossary.html#thermal-headroom","w":"getThermalHeadroom thermal state ProcessInfo.thermalState thermal status"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Thermal throttling","s":"A device lowering its chips' clock speeds when it gets too hot, which makes a game slow down partway through a session.","u":"glossary.html#thermal-throttling","w":"throttling thermal limiting"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Thickness heuristic","s":"A guess that surfaces in the depth buffer are only so thick, so geometry behind them is treated as open. It reduces dark halos around thin…","u":"glossary.html#thickness-heuristic","w":"thickness"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Thread group","s":"A block of compute shader threads, such as 8 × 8, that run together on one compute unit and can share fast memory. A dispatch launches a…","u":"glossary.html#thread-group","w":"workgroup work group numthreads dispatch"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Update","s":"The step in each frame where the engine moves the game forward by the time that has passed: it reads input, runs gameplay code and updates…","u":"glossary.html#tick","w":"tick Tick() Update() _process()"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tile-based GPU","s":"A GPU that sorts a pass's triangles into small screen tiles, then renders each tile in fast on-chip memory and writes it out once. Phone…","u":"glossary.html#tile-based-gpu","w":"tile-based rendering TBR tile-based deferred rendering TBDR tiler"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tile memory","s":"The small, fast memory inside a tile-based GPU that holds one tile's colour, depth and samples while it is drawn. Reading and writing it…","u":"glossary.html#tile-memory","w":"on-chip tile memory tile buffer GMEM imageblock memory local tile memory"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tiled shading","s":"Light culling that cuts the screen into small tiles, such as 32 × 32 pixels, and lists the lights reaching each tile, so each pixel loops…","u":"glossary.html#tiled-shading","w":"tiled deferred tiled forward tiled lighting FPTL"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Time-slicing","s":"Spreading periodic or large work over several frames under a per-frame limit, such as updating a quarter of the characters each frame, so…","u":"glossary.html#time-slicing","w":"amortising staggered updates frame budgeting time limit per frame"},
  {"k":"term","t":"TLAS","s":"The acceleration structure over a scene's instances: each entry points at a BLAS and stores the instance's transform, ID and mask. Engines…","u":"glossary.html#tlas","w":"top-level acceleration structure top-level AS instance acceleration structure"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tone curve","s":"The function a tone mapper applies: a dark toe, a nearly straight middle and a shoulder that rolls highlights off towards white instead of…","u":"glossary.html#tone-curve","w":"tone mapping curve toe shoulder S-curve"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tone mapping","s":"Turning the frame's scene light, which can be thousands of times brighter than white, into values a screen can show, with a curve that…","u":"glossary.html#tone-mapping","w":"tonemapping tone mapper tonemapper view transform display transform"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tool use","s":"An AI model asking a program to do something for it, such as searching code, building, running tests or summarising a capture, then…","u":"glossary.html#tool-use","w":"tool calling function calling tools"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Tools programmer","s":"A programmer who builds the editors, importers, build systems and automation that the rest of the team uses to make the game.","u":"glossary.html#tools-programmer","w":"tools engineer pipeline programmer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Toon shading","s":"A stylised shading model that cuts smooth lighting into a few flat bands, usually with hard-edged highlights and ink outlines, so 3D…","u":"glossary.html#toon-shading","w":"cel shading cel-shading anime shading"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Topology","s":"How a mesh's corners, edges and faces connect to each other, whatever their exact positions. Clean topology is easy to edit, unwrap and…","u":"glossary.html#topology","w":"mesh topology edge flow edge loops"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Training data provenance","s":"Where the data used to train an AI model came from and on what terms. It affects the legal risk of using the model's output, which is a…","u":"glossary.html#training-data-provenance","w":"data provenance provenance training data licensing"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Transform","s":"An object's position, rotation and scale. Every object placed in a game world has one.","u":"glossary.html#transform","w":"position, rotation and scale TRS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Transform hierarchy","s":"Parent and child links between objects, so that moving, turning or scaling a parent does the same to all of its children.","u":"glossary.html#transform-hierarchy","w":"parent-child parenting attachment"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Transmission","s":"Light passing right through a thin part of a surface, such as a leaf, an ear or a curtain, and lighting its far side. Shaders estimate it…","u":"glossary.html#transmission","w":"thin-surface translucency thin translucency backlight Two Sided Foliage"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Transparency sorting","s":"Drawing transparent objects after all opaque ones, ordered from the furthest to the nearest, usually by the distance to each object's…","u":"glossary.html#transparency-sorting","w":"sort order translucency sort back-to-front sorting sort priority"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Transparents pass","s":"The part of a frame that draws see-through surfaces such as glass, water and particles after the opaque scene is lit, sorted back to front…","u":"glossary.html#transparents-pass","w":"transparent pass translucency pass Translucency forward transparent pass"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Traversal stutter","s":"Hitches that appear as the player moves through a streamed world, usually when a newly loaded area is made live on the game thread all at…","u":"glossary.html#traversal-stutter","w":"streaming stutter streaming hitch cell activation hitch"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Triangle","s":"The basic shape GPUs draw. Three corners always lie on one flat plane, which makes triangles fast and unambiguous to fill.","u":"glossary.html#triangle","w":"polygon face primitive"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Triangle list","s":"The most common way to read indices: every three indices form one independent triangle.","u":"glossary.html#triangle-list","w":"primitive topology"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Triangulation","s":"Splitting quads and n-gons into triangles, on export, on import or in the engine. Doing it once, at a known point, keeps the triangles…","u":"glossary.html#triangulation","w":"triangulate triangulated"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Trilinear filtering","s":"Bilinear filtering in the two nearest mip levels, blended by where the pixel's size falls between them. It hides the lines where one mip…","u":"glossary.html#trilinear-filtering","w":"trilinear linear mipmap filtering Tri-linear"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Trim sheet","s":"A texture made of horizontal strips of reusable detail, such as edges, seams and pipes, that tile along U. Many meshes lay their UVs over…","u":"glossary.html#trim-sheet","w":"trim texture trims"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Triple buffering","s":"Using three framebuffers so the GPU can start the next frame while a finished one waits for the display. It keeps the GPU busy under vsync…","u":"glossary.html#triple-buffering","w":"three-buffer swap chain"},
  {"k":"term","t":"TSR","s":"Unreal Engine 5's built-in temporal upscaler and anti-aliasing, on by default. It runs on any Shader Model 5 GPU and on current consoles,…","u":"glossary.html#tsr","w":"Temporal Super Resolution r.AntiAliasingMethod 4"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Ubershader","s":"One large shader that handles many features with branches instead of separate compiled variants. It needs fewer compiles but usually runs…","u":"glossary.html#ubershader","w":"uber shader mega shader"},
  {"k":"term","t":"UI compositing","s":"Drawing the game's interface over the finished 3D image as the last step before present, at the display's own resolution and after every…","u":"glossary.html#ui-compositing","w":"UI pass HUD pass interface pass UI overlay"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Uncompressed format","s":"A texture format that stores every texel in full, such as RGBA8 at 4 bytes per texel. Exact, but four to eight times larger than…","u":"glossary.html#uncompressed-format","w":"RGBA8 RGBA32 R8G8B8A8 RGBA16F"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unified memory","s":"One pool of memory shared by the CPU and GPU, as on consoles, phones and headsets, instead of separate system RAM and VRAM as on most PCs.","u":"glossary.html#unified-memory","w":"shared memory UMA"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unit throughput","s":"How busy one GPU unit was during a pass, as a percentage of the most it could do. The unit with the highest reading is the likely limit:…","u":"glossary.html#unit-throughput","w":"SOL% speed of light peak-performance percentage throughput %"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unit vector","s":"A vector with a length of exactly 1, so it carries only a direction. Normalising a vector divides it by its own length to make it one.","u":"glossary.html#unit-vector","w":"normalised vector normalized vector normalise normalize direction"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unity","s":"Unity Technologies' engine, scripted in C#, with two current render pipelines, URP and HDRP. It is licensed per seat and free for small…","u":"glossary.html#unity","w":"Unity 6 Unity engine"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unity Frame Debugger","s":"Unity's built-in frame debugger. It lists the rendering events of one frame and shows the scene's state after each, in the Editor or…","u":"glossary.html#unity-frame-debugger","w":"Frame Debugger"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unity Profiler","s":"Unity's Profiler window: modules that chart CPU, GPU, rendering, memory and other data per frame, recorded from Play mode in the Editor…","u":"glossary.html#unity-profiler","w":"Profiler window CPU Usage module GPU Usage module"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unreal Engine","s":"Epic Games' engine, written in C++ with Blueprint visual scripting. Its fifth version brought features such as Nanite geometry and Lumen…","u":"glossary.html#unreal-engine","w":"UE5 UE Unreal"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Unreal Insights","s":"Unreal Engine's tool for recording and analysing trace data from a running game, including CPU threads and GPU passes frame by frame,…","u":"glossary.html#unreal-insights","w":"Insights Timing Insights utrace Trace"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Up axis","s":"The axis a coordinate system treats as vertical. Unity, Godot and glTF use Y; Unreal Engine, Blender and 3ds Max use Z.","u":"glossary.html#up-axis","w":"Y-up Z-up vertical axis"},
  {"k":"term","t":"URP","s":"Unity's render pipeline for every platform Unity supports, tuned for mobile GPUs and standalone VR. Unity recommends it for new projects.","u":"glossary.html#urp","w":"Universal Render Pipeline"},
  {"k":"term","t":"USD","s":"Pixar's open-source scene description format, built for assembling large scenes from many files. Layers, references and variants let teams…","u":"glossary.html#usd","w":"OpenUSD Universal Scene Description .usda .usdc .usdz"},
  {"k":"term","t":"UV coordinates","s":"Two numbers per vertex saying which point of a 2D texture lands on that corner of the 3D surface.","u":"glossary.html#uv","w":"UVs texture coordinates texcoords"},
  {"k":"term","t":"UV island","s":"One connected flat piece of a mesh's UV layout, bounded by seams. Islands are packed into the texture with gaps between them.","u":"glossary.html#uv-island","w":"UV shell UV chart island"},
  {"k":"term","t":"UV seam","s":"An edge where the UV layout is cut so the surface can lie flat in the texture. The triangles on either side land in different places in…","u":"glossary.html#uv-seam","w":"seam texture seam UV cut"},
  {"k":"term","t":"UV unwrapping","s":"Giving each vertex of a mesh its UV coordinates by cutting the surface along seams, flattening the pieces and packing them into the…","u":"glossary.html#uv-unwrap","w":"unwrap UV mapping UV layout"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Variable hardware","s":"A platform where players own very different machines, such as PCs and Android phones, so a game must adapt its settings to each one.","u":"glossary.html#variable-hardware","w":"open platform hardware fragmentation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vector","s":"An arrow written as a list of numbers, one per axis: (3, 2) means 3 along x and 2 along y. It has a direction and a length but no fixed…","u":"glossary.html#vector","w":"direction vector displacement float3 Vector3 FVector"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Verification plan","s":"How a result will be checked and by whom: which tests run, which captures, timings and screenshots are compared, on which hardware, and…","u":"glossary.html#verification-plan","w":"test plan how we will check validation plan"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Version control","s":"A system that keeps every revision of every file, records who changed what and when, and lets a team return to any earlier state.","u":"glossary.html#version-control","w":"source control revision control VCS"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex","s":"One corner of a mesh together with everything stored about it: position, normal, UVs and more.","u":"glossary.html#vertex","w":"vertices vert"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex animation","s":"Moving a mesh's vertices in the vertex shader every frame, for effects such as foliage swaying in the wind or a flag rippling, with no…","u":"glossary.html#vertex-animation","w":"vertex shader animation World Position Offset WPO vertex offset vertex displacement"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex animation texture","s":"Animation baked into a texture: each texel holds one vertex's position in one frame, and the vertex shader reads the row for the current…","u":"glossary.html#vertex-animation-texture","w":"VAT baked vertex animation AnimToTexture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex attribute","s":"One piece of data stored per vertex, such as position, normal, UV, colour or bone weights.","u":"glossary.html#vertex-attribute","w":"attribute vertex channel vertex element"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex buffer","s":"A block of GPU memory holding a mesh's vertices as rows of numbers, one row per vertex, ready for the GPU to read.","u":"glossary.html#vertex-buffer","w":"VBO vertex array"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Post-transform vertex cache","s":"A small GPU cache of recently processed vertices. When an index repeats soon after, the GPU reuses the result instead of running the…","u":"glossary.html#vertex-cache","w":"vertex cache post-transform cache"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex colour","s":"A colour, or four numbers, stored at each vertex and blended across the triangles between. Games use it as a cheap mask for dirt, material…","u":"glossary.html#vertex-colour","w":"vertex color vertex paint COLOR_0"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex layout","s":"The description that tells the GPU how to read a vertex buffer: which attributes it holds, their formats, and their byte offsets inside…","u":"glossary.html#vertex-layout","w":"input layout vertex declaration vertex format vertex descriptor"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex shader","s":"The GPU program that runs once per vertex, typically to move it from the model's own coordinates to its place on screen.","u":"glossary.html#vertex-shader","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertex stream","s":"One of several vertex buffers that together describe a mesh, each holding some of the attributes, such as positions in one buffer and…","u":"glossary.html#vertex-stream","w":"separate streams structure of arrays SoA"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertical blank","s":"The short pause after a display has drawn its bottom row and before it starts again at the top. Swapping frames during it causes no tearing.","u":"glossary.html#vertical-blank","w":"vblank vertical blanking interval VBI"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vertical slice","s":"A small part of a game, such as one level, built to final quality in every layer and running on the target hardware. It proves the game…","u":"glossary.html#vertical-slice","w":"slice"},
  {"k":"term","t":"VFX artist","s":"An artist who creates visual effects such as fire, smoke, magic, impacts and weather, usually with particle systems and custom materials.","u":"glossary.html#vfx-artist","w":"effects artist FX artist"},
  {"k":"term","t":"View matrix","s":"The matrix that moves the whole world into view space so the camera sits at the origin. It is the inverse of the camera's own transform.","u":"glossary.html#view-matrix","w":"camera matrix world-to-view matrix world-to-camera matrix V"},
  {"k":"term","t":"View space","s":"Coordinates measured from the camera: the camera sits at the origin and looks straight along one axis. Some lighting and depth…","u":"glossary.html#view-space","w":"camera space eye space"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vignette","s":"Darkening towards the corners of the image, as many lenses do, used to draw the eye to the centre.","u":"glossary.html#vignette","w":"vignetting"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Virtual shadow maps","s":"Very large shadow maps, 16,384 texels square in Unreal Engine 5, cut into pages that are allocated, rendered and cached only where…","u":"glossary.html#virtual-shadow-maps","w":"VSM VSMs paged shadow maps"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Virtual texturing","s":"Cutting textures into small pages and loading only the pages visible pixels use into a fixed cache, with a page table telling the shader…","u":"glossary.html#virtual-texturing","w":"SVT streaming virtual texturing runtime virtual texturing megatexture"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Visibility buffer","s":"A screen buffer that stores only which triangle covers each pixel, in as few as 4 bytes. A later pass looks the triangle up, rebuilds the…","u":"glossary.html#visibility-buffer","w":"vis buffer VisBuffer"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Volumetric clouds","s":"Clouds rendered by ray marching a 3D density field, usually built from tiling noise shaped by a weather map, between two altitudes. Lit by…","u":"glossary.html#volumetric-clouds","w":"Volumetric Cloud Volumetric Clouds ray-marched clouds Nubis"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Volumetric fog","s":"Fog computed in a 3D grid that fills the view, so its density can vary from place to place and every light, with its shadows, can light…","u":"glossary.html#volumetric-fog","w":"volumetric lighting Volumetric Fog volumetric scattering"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Voxel cone tracing","s":"Real-time GI that stores the scene's lit surfaces in voxels with blurred, coarser copies, then gathers light along a few cones per pixel,…","u":"glossary.html#voxel-cone-tracing","w":"VCT VXGI SVOGI sparse voxel octree global illumination"},
  {"k":"term","t":"VoxelGI","s":"Godot's voxel-based GI node. It voxelizes static geometry inside a box in a bake of seconds, then lights it in real time, so lights can…","u":"glossary.html#voxelgi","w":"VoxelGI node"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Reprojection (VR)","s":"A headset reusing or synthesising frames when the game's frame is late or deliberately rendered at half rate, warping images to the latest…","u":"glossary.html#vr-reprojection","w":"reprojection TimeWarp SpaceWarp Application SpaceWarp AppSW"},
  {"k":"term","t":"VRAM","s":"The GPU's own memory. Meshes and textures live here so the GPU can read them fast without asking the CPU.","u":"glossary.html#vram","w":"video RAM video memory GPU memory"},
  {"k":"term","t":"VRAM over-commit","s":"A PC game using more GPU memory than its budget. Windows may freeze it at times or leave resources in slower system memory, so frames slow…","u":"glossary.html#vram-overcommit","w":"VRAM oversubscription over the VRAM budget memory oversubscription paging to system memory"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Variable refresh rate","s":"A display mode in which the screen waits for each new frame and refreshes when it arrives, within a range such as 48 to 144 Hz. It removes…","u":"glossary.html#vrr","w":"VRR G-SYNC FreeSync Adaptive-Sync HDMI VRR"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Variable rate shading","s":"A GPU feature that runs the pixel shader once for a block of pixels, such as 2 by 2, in chosen parts of the screen, while depth and…","u":"glossary.html#vrs","w":"VRS coarse pixel shading shading rate image fragment shading rate"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vsync","s":"Holding each finished frame until the display's vertical blank before swapping it in. It removes tearing but adds waiting, and uneven…","u":"glossary.html#vsync","w":"V-Sync vertical sync vertical synchronisation"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vulkan","s":"The Khronos Group's cross-platform explicit graphics API. It is the main API on Android and Linux, including the Steam Deck, and also runs…","u":"glossary.html#vulkan","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"Vulkan ray tracing","s":"The Khronos extensions, final since November 2020, that add acceleration structures, ray tracing pipelines and ray queries to Vulkan.","u":"glossary.html#vulkan-ray-tracing","w":"VK_KHR_ray_tracing_pipeline VK_KHR_acceleration_structure VK_KHR_ray_query"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Warp","s":"A group of GPU lanes that run the same instruction together: 32 lanes on NVIDIA GPUs, 32 or 64 on AMD's. When lanes in one warp take…","u":"glossary.html#warp","w":"wavefront wave subgroup SIMD-group"},
  {"k":"term","t":"WebGPU","s":"The modern graphics API for web browsers and the successor to WebGL. Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari ship it, though not yet on every…","u":"glossary.html#webgpu","w":""},
  {"k":"term","t":"White balance","s":"Shifting colours so that a chosen light reads as neutral white, removing a warm or cool cast, as cameras do. Engines expose it as…","u":"glossary.html#white-balance","w":"Temperature and Tint Temp and Tint white point"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Winding order","s":"Whether a triangle's corners go clockwise or anticlockwise on screen. The GPU uses it to decide which side of the triangle is the front.","u":"glossary.html#winding-order","w":"front face CW clockwise CCW counter-clockwise"},
  {"k":"term","t":"World Partition","s":"Unreal Engine's open-world system: the world is divided into grid cells that are loaded and unloaded automatically around streaming…","u":"glossary.html#world-partition","w":"runtime grid streaming grid World Partition grid"},
  {"k":"term","t":"World space","s":"The single coordinate space shared by the whole level, in which every object, light and camera has a position.","u":"glossary.html#world-space","w":"global space scene space"},
  {"k":"term","t":"World transform","s":"An object's position, rotation and scale measured from the world's origin, found by combining its local transform with every parent above…","u":"glossary.html#world-transform","w":"global transform global_position world location"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Wrap mode","s":"What the GPU does with UV coordinates outside 0 to 1: repeat the texture, clamp to its edge texels, or mirror it on every repeat.","u":"glossary.html#wrap-mode","w":"address mode tiling method repeat clamp mirror"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Metal debugger","s":"Xcode's tools for capturing and inspecting Metal GPU work on Apple platforms: a frame's commands, resources and dependencies, a…","u":"glossary.html#xcode-metal-debugger","w":"Xcode Metal debugger GPU frame capture Metal capture gputrace gpudebug"},
  {"k":"term","t":"XeSS","s":"Intel's machine-learned temporal upscaler, using the matrix units of Arc GPUs with a cross-vendor fallback, plus XeSS Frame Generation and…","u":"glossary.html#xess","w":"Intel Xe Super Sampling XeSS-SR XeSS-FG XeLL Xe Low Latency"},
  {"k":"term","t":"Z-fighting","s":"Flickering stripes where two surfaces closer together than one step of stored depth show through each other, because rounding rather than…","u":"glossary.html#z-fighting","w":"depth fighting z-fight"}
